  user_input {
    user_response: "Build a production-quality Android app called **NetGuardian** \342\200\224 a privacy-focused per-app network firewall and background activity controller.\n\n## Core Purpose\n\nThe app allows the user to control internet access for individual Android applications and reduce unwanted background network activity to improve privacy, reduce mobile-data usage, and potentially improve battery life.\n\nThe app must use Android\'s **VpnService** API for non-root traffic filtering. Do not create fake UI toggles that only change visual state.\n\n## Main Features\n\n### 1. Dashboard\n\nCreate a modern dashboard showing:\n\n* VPN firewall status: ON/OFF\n* Number of blocked applications\n* Number of allowed applications\n* Data blocked today\n* Active network connections\n* Current Wi-Fi/mobile-data status\n\nProvide a prominent **Start Firewall / Stop Firewall** button.\n\nWhen firewall protection is active, show a persistent status indicator.\n\n### 2. Installed Apps\n\nCreate an application manager that loads installed Android applications dynamically.\n\nFor every application display:\n\n* App icon\n* App name\n* Package name\n* Firewall status\n* Wi-Fi access\n* Mobile-data access\n* Background access\n\nEach app should have a detailed configuration screen.\n\nExample:\n\nYouTube\n\nWi-Fi\n[ ON ]\n\nMobile Data\n[ OFF ]\n\nBackground Network\n[ OFF ]\n\nBlock Trackers\n[ ON ]\n\n### 3. Per-App Network Rules\n\nSupport these rules:\n\n* Allow all network traffic\n* Block all network traffic\n* Allow Wi-Fi only\n* Allow mobile data only\n* Block background traffic\n* Block while screen is off\n\nRules must be persisted locally using Room.\n\nThe firewall engine should identify traffic by Android application UID wherever Android APIs permit this.\n\n### 4. VPN Firewall Engine\n\nImplement the firewall using Android `VpnService`.\n\nArchitecture:\n\nUI\n\342\206\222 Firewall Manager\n\342\206\222 VpnService\n\342\206\222 packet/connection filtering\n\342\206\222 network\n\nThe VPN service must:\n\n* Create a local VPN interface\n* Route traffic through the filtering engine\n* Apply application-specific rules\n* Allow permitted traffic\n* Drop blocked traffic\n* Log blocked connection attempts\n* Avoid sending user traffic to a remote server\n\nThe firewall should work completely locally.\n\nClearly handle Android limitations where application-level traffic filtering cannot be guaranteed.\n\n### 5. Connection Log\n\nCreate a live connection log.\n\nEach event should show:\n\n* Application\n* Destination address/domain when available\n* Protocol\n* Port\n* Allowed/Blocked\n* Timestamp\n\nExample:\n\nYouTube\ngooglevideo.com\n443\nBLOCKED\n01:24 AM\n\n### 6. Privacy Protection\n\nAdd optional DNS-based filtering.\n\nAllow the user to enable:\n\n* Ad blocking\n* Tracker blocking\n* Malware-domain blocking\n* Custom blocklists\n\nProvide a simple rule management screen.\n\nDo not hardcode a third-party server as mandatory.\n\n### 7. Background Activity Control\n\nCreate a dedicated Background Activity section.\n\nFor each application:\n\n* Allow background network\n* Block background network\n* Block when screen is OFF\n* Block when device is idle\n\nExplain clearly that Android\'s exact background execution/network behavior varies by Android version and manufacturer.\n\nDo not claim that the application can forcibly stop every background process without privileged/root/system permissions.\n\n### 8. Quick Controls\n\nAdd quick controls for:\n\n* Firewall ON/OFF\n* Block all non-system apps\n* Allow all apps\n* Wi-Fi-only mode\n* Mobile-data-only mode\n\nUse Android-supported mechanisms such as a notification and, where appropriate, a Quick Settings tile.\n\n### 9. Battery/Data Statistics\n\nShow:\n\n* Estimated blocked connections\n* Network activity by application\n* Data usage information where Android APIs permit it\n* Daily/weekly statistics\n\nUse charts and clean cards.\n\nDo not fabricate network statistics if Android does not provide the required information.\n\n### 10. App Settings\n\nInclude:\n\n* Start firewall on boot\n* Persistent notification\n* Auto-start firewall\n* Default rule for newly installed apps\n* Dark/light/system theme\n* DNS filtering\n* Blocklist management\n* Export settings\n* Import settings\n* Reset firewall rules\n\n### 11. UI/UX\n\nDesign a polished modern Android interface.\n\nUse:\n\n* Material 3\n* Jetpack Compose\n* Dark mode\n* Light mode\n* Dynamic color where supported\n* Smooth animations\n* Bottom navigation\n\nNavigation:\n\nHome\nApps\nConnections\nPrivacy\nSettings\n\nThe UI should feel similar to a professional privacy/security application rather than a generic generated app.\n\n### 12. Security and Privacy Principles\n\nThe app must be privacy-first.\n\nImportant requirements:\n\n* No account required\n* No cloud backend required\n* No telemetry\n* No advertising SDK\n* No user traffic uploaded to a server\n* Store rules locally\n* Minimize permissions\n* Explain every sensitive permission to the user\n\n### 13. Android Restrictions\n\nBe technically honest about Android\'s security model.\n\nDo NOT pretend that a normal application can directly toggle another application\'s system-level Wi-Fi/mobile-data permission.\n\nUse `VpnService` for non-root firewall functionality.\n\nFor capabilities unavailable to ordinary apps, clearly indicate:\n\n\"Requires root or privileged/system access.\"\n\nAvoid requesting unnecessary accessibility, device-admin, root, or other privileged permissions.\n\n### 14. Architecture\n\nUse a maintainable architecture:\n\n* Kotlin\n* Jetpack Compose\n* MVVM or Clean Architecture\n* Room\n* DataStore\n* Kotlin Coroutines\n* Android VpnService\n* WorkManager where appropriate\n\nSuggested structure:\n\napp/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ui/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 home/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 apps/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 connections/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 privacy/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 settings/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 firewall/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 FirewallService\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 FirewallManager\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RuleEngine\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 ConnectionLogger\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 database/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 AppRule\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ConnectionLog\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Dao\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 dns/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 permissions/\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 utils/\n\n### 15. Important Implementation Requirement\n\nPrioritize actual functionality over mock screens.\n\nFor every UI control that claims to enable/disable networking, connect it to real application logic.\n\nIf a requested capability cannot be implemented with public Android APIs, do not simulate it. Instead:\n\n1. Implement the closest technically correct functionality.\n2. Explain the limitation in the UI.\n3. Structure the code so privileged/root functionality could be added later.\n\nStart by generating the complete project architecture and working core firewall implementation, then build the UI around it.\n for managing network permissions with an intuitive dashboard."
