  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Addressing Image Generation Failure**\n\nI\'ve hit the `generate_image` quota limit. The plan now is to manually craft a custom Vector Drawable for the app\'s foreground icon. The app-icon-generation skill recommends picking a bold, filled icon from the core Material Icons library as the fallback. I\'ll need to focus on implementing this solution now.\n\n\n**Defining App Architecture**\n\nI am now delving into the architectural design of NetGuardian. It\'s becoming clearer. The UI will feed into a Firewall Manager, which in turn interfaces with a VpnService. This service is the heart of the system, responsible for filtering packets/connections and applying application-specific rules, routing traffic through the filtering engine, and controlling network access. I will need to build the custom assets as well.\n\n\n**Analyzing VPN Implementation**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on the operational aspects of the VPN implementation. The aim is local operation. My strategy involves permitting specific traffic while dropping and logging blocked attempts. I\'m focusing on locally managing the firewall and tackling Android\'s app-level traffic filtering limitations. Specifically, I\'m examining how to configure `VpnService.Builder`, including address setup, routing, DNS, and session settings, particularly leveraging the per-app capabilities available on API 21+ for allowing or disallowing apps\' VPN use.\n\n\n**Clarifying Traffic Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining the packet handling strategy. It\'s becoming clearer that I need to examine the two main Android firewall models. Model A involves routing all apps through the VPN interface and selectively handling them. I\'m leaning toward Model B, which leverages `addAllowedApplication` and `addDisallowedApplication` for a more robust approach. By selectively including applications within the VPN, and then NOT forwarding them, they can be blocked completely, while others will be allowed access.\n\n\n**Investigating Packet Inspection**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the nitty-gritty of packet analysis. I\'m excited about the possibilities! Specifically, I\'m figuring out how to inspect and log connections by examining the packet data using a `FileInputStream` on the `tunInterface.fileDescriptor`. I can parse IP headers and identify packets. This looks like a solid approach. Now, I will look for ways to handle DNS queries and blocklists.\n\n\n**Mapping Network Rules**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on correlating network rules with app permissions and states. I\'ll log connection attempts, capturing app name, package, and protocol. I\'m also addressing screen-off, device idle, and network changes to apply rules dynamically via `BroadcastReceiver` and `NetworkCallback`. Background network activity is handled too via process state monitoring and lifecycle observation.\n\n\n\n**Defining Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve formalized the data structures needed for rule management and logging. I\'m defining `AppRuleEntity` to track app-specific network policies including package name, blocking criteria, and notes, and `ConnectionLogEntity` to record connection details with block reasons. I\'ve also added a `BlocklistEntity` for privacy protection with domains and categories. I\'m now implementing these entities to support the application.\n\n\n\n**Defining App Configuration**\n\nI\'m now outlining key preference settings. I\'ve designed settings for global blocking of trackers, ads, and malware. Additionally, I\'m integrating options for start-on-boot, persistent notifications, and a default block for new apps. I\'m also including quick-block options for all apps, non-system apps, Wi-Fi only, and mobile only. Finally, I\'m planning for a theme mode selection.\n\n\n**Designing App Display**\n\nI\'m now focused on the UI elements for installed app display and network rule management. I will implement a log preview card with navigation to full logs. I\'ll dynamically load installed apps with their icons, names, and package details, filtering options, quick toggles, and detailed per-app configuration screens for network rules. I\'ll persist the rules in the Room database, and the engine will dynamically re-evaluate firewall states.\n\n\n**Implementing Connection Logging**\n\nI\'m now implementing the connection logs, and I\'m quite happy with my progress. I\'m focusing on the live stream via Room Flow and detailed filtering options. I\'m excited to include the live stream of connection events with detailed information. I\'m adding filters for various event types, details with badges, and clear/export actions. I\'m also addressing the privacy protection features with DNS-based filtering and built-in curated lists to start.\n\n\n**Refining App Control Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating controls for background activity. I\'ll provide a dedicated section explaining Android\'s background execution limitations. I\'m focusing on allowing per-app control for network blocking, and background control during screen off or device idle states. I\'ll also add technical disclosure and quick controls, as well as add battery and data statistics to the display.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 595974046
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
