  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Background Elements**\n\nI\'ve been exploring how to add a dedicated \"Background Activity\" section. My latest thought involves using `Screen.Background` with options like `SyncDisabled`, `HourglassTop`, or `Sensors` to create a first-class citizen for managing background processes within the system. This seems like a promising direction to give it a UI-level status that is separate from traditional foreground UI objects.\n\n\n**Refining User Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the user interface for the background activity section, specifically the bottom bar. I\'m exploring how users will switch to this section directly and also considering the specific toggle options required for each application. These options include: \'Allow background network,\' \'Block background network,\' \'Block when screen is off,\' and \'Block when device is idle.\' I\'m also preparing to create educational materials explaining Android\'s background execution limitations based on OS version and manufacturer.\n\n\n**Analyzing OS Behavior**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the impact of different Android versions, specifically focusing on how they handle background processes. I\'m noting key changes like the introduction of App Standby Buckets in Android 9-11, the Phantom Process Killer in Android 12, and mandatory foreground service declarations in Android 13. I\'m also examining how these variations are influenced by OEM aggressive task killers, such as those found in Xiaomi, Samsung, and Huawei devices.\n\n\n**Investigating Device Variations**\n\nI\'m now investigating OEM-specific behaviors concerning background processes. I\'m noting that some, like Oppo, OnePlus, and Realme (ColorOS, OxygenOS), implement deep optimizations and standby restrictions, while Google Pixel sticks with standard Doze/Standby Buckets. Notably, \"PowerGenie\" daemons kill processes without warning. Finally, I\'m examining how NetGuardian can be a good design solution.\n\n\n**Implementing UI Controls**\n\nI\'m now designing the user interface for background process management. Each application will have toggle controls within its card or expanded sheet to precisely manage network access and execution states: \'Allow background network\'/\'Block background network\', \'Block when screen is off\', and \'Block when device is idle\'. I\'ll add a quick status badge to provide visual feedback and display app details. I\'m verifying that `AppRuleEntity.kt` has fields for those. I have to verify Room db schema changes if `AppRuleEntity` changes.\n\n\n**Verifying Database Changes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on database schema migrations. I\'ve confirmed that incrementing the `AppDatabase.kt` version or using `.fallbackToDestructiveMigration()` handles schema mismatches effectively. I\'m checking how the `RuleEngine.kt` uses the `blockDeviceIdle` flag to block execution during idle states using `PowerManager.isDeviceIdleMode`, specifically in `FirewallVpnService` / `FirewallManager`, which reacts to `PowerManager.ACTION_DEVICE_IDLE_MODE_CHANGED`. This approach is confirmed to be a legitimate Android API behavior.\n\n\n**Designing Filtering Implementation**\n\nI\'m now outlining DNS-based filtering implementation details. I\'ll include global and individual category toggles (Ads, Trackers, Malware) within `PreferencesRepository` and `PrivacyScreen`. I will have an option to manage custom blocklists with search, add/delete functionality, and import capabilities.\n\n\n**Defining DNS Resolver**\n\nI\'m now detailing the DNS-based filtering implementation, focusing on custom blocklist management. I\'ll support export, clear, add, delete, import (from plaintext/hosts/JSON), and global category toggles (Ads, Trackers, Malware) within the UI. I will confirm that all features function locally. I\'ll design a pure on-device DNS sinkholing, immediately responding with a 0.0.0.0 or NXDOMAIN without an internet roundtrip for blocked domains intercepted via `FirewallVpnService`. I\'ll allow users to configure upstream DNS resolvers with system defaults, loopback, or custom IPs in settings.\n\n\n**Adding Public Resolvers**\n\nI\'m now integrating common public DNS resolvers as presets, such as Cloudflare, Quad9, and Google, while ensuring no external server is mandatory. The DNS filtering itself remains entirely local.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'m now moving to implement quick controls for NetGuardian within a persistent notification and Quick Settings tile as per requirement, and have analyzed the Notification limitations, specifically the availability of interactive action buttons.\n\n\n**Implementing Quick Controls**\n\nI\'m now implementing persistent notifications and Quick Settings tile controls. Actions like \"Toggle Firewall\", \"Block Non-System\", \"Wi-Fi Only\", and \"Allow All\" will trigger intents to `FirewallVpnService`, updating the firewall manager and refreshing the UI. This provides full, real-time control. I\'m also confirming the `FirewallTileService` is properly declared.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 114868611
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
