  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Requirements Details**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the specific NetGuardian requirements, particularly the \"Background Activity\" section. I\'m focusing on user-toggleable options within each application, such as the crucial \"Allow Background Activity\" feature. The goal is clarity and user-friendliness, ensuring all aspects are considered.\n\n\n**Defining Background Screen Structure**\n\nI\'m now outlining the structure of the `BackgroundActivityScreen.kt` file. I\'ll include a top-level overview of NetGuardian\'s background activity controls, followed by an interactive guide. This guide will focus on Android\'s version-specific and manufacturer-specific limitations, like Xiaomi\'s aggressive task-killing. It\'s crucial to inform users about background execution restrictions and provide practical context for NetGuardian\'s functionality. The design will integrate collapsible cards to allow users to dive deep.\n\n\n**Analyzing Device-Specific Issues**\n\nI\'m now focusing on device-specific background activity limitations. Samsung\'s \"Deep Sleeping Apps\" cause a full suspension. Huawei, OnePlus, and Oppo have their own aggressive power management. Understanding how Android Security Sandbox, and VPNservice works is also key.\n\nI am finalizing the direct shortcut for \"Battery Optimization\" settings to help users. I\'m also implementing an app list with a search bar and filters, which are key for practical NetGuardian control.\n\n\n**Implementing Background Network Controls**\n\nI\'m now implementing granular control over background network usage for each app. I\'m introducing toggle switches for \"Allow/Block background network\", \"Block when screen is off,\" and \"Block when device is idle\" within the app card and the bottom sheet. This is crucial for user control. I\'m focusing on \"User Apps\" and \"System Apps\" separately. Batch action controls like \"Block All Background\" and \"Allow All Background\" are also included for efficiency.\n\n**Adding DNS Filtering Feature**\n\nI am now implementing optional DNS-based filtering functionality within NetGuardian. I\'ve included a master toggle switch in `PrivacyScreen.kt` to enable or disable the feature. Users will be able to block ads, trackers, and malware domains locally, with the option to customize blocklists. This feature is intended to be completely local, without relying on any external servers.\n\n\n**Adding DNS Filtering Details**\n\nI\'m now expanding DNS filtering. I\'m adding toggles for ad, tracker, and malware blocking, with real-time domain counts. I am making it mandatory that no third-party server be hardcoded. The user can pick Local Loopback/Pure Offline Sinkhole, System Default, or Custom DNS Resolver (with public privacy resolvers as an option). I\'m also planning a simple rule management screen, including viewing, searching, adding, toggling, deleting, importing, exporting, and clearing rules. It\'s all locally implemented via `DnsPacketParser.buildSinkholeResponse`.\n\n\n**Implementing Quick Settings Tile**\n\nI\'m now integrating a Quick Settings tile for firewall control within `FirewallTileService.kt`, supporting Android N (API 24) and later. It displays the current firewall state and mode, allowing toggling ON/OFF and cycling through modes. The long-press will control mode switching with dynamic labels like \"Firewall: ON\" and \"Mode: Wi-Fi Only,\" alongside actions to open Quick Controls. A persistent notification in `FirewallVpnService.kt` is also being developed for accessibility.\n\n\n**Designing Notification Control**\n\nI\'m now focusing on interactive action buttons for the persistent notification, with actions to toggle the firewall, block non-system apps, and switch between Wi-Fi and mobile modes. I\'m using PendingIntents to send these actions to `FirewallVpnService`, and on receiving these actions, I\'ll execute the change, re-establish VPN filtering, and update the persistent notification with the new state! I\'m also planning Quick Control toggles to be displayed in the app\'s UI.\n\n\n**Verifying Rule Database Structure**\n\nI\'m now carefully reviewing `AppRuleEntity.kt` to ensure I\'ve correctly incorporated the new boolean flag `blockDeviceIdle`. I\'m confident that the database schema includes all the necessary fields for fine-grained network control within NetGuardian, including the ability to block when the device is idle. I\'m taking the opportunity to increment the database version to account for this change, ensuring a smooth transition.\n\n\n**Refining Database Updates**\n\nI\'m now implementing database version upgrades, adding `.fallbackToDestructiveMigration()` or a migration. I\'m also confirming the inclusion of `blockDeviceIdle` across the database schema. In `FirewallDao.kt`, I\'m adding queries to reset all rules and control `blockBackground`, `blockScreenOff`, and `blockDeviceIdle`. And in `PreferencesRepository.kt`, I am adding upstream DNS preferences.\n\n\n**Defining DNS Selection Options**\n\nI\'m now integrating upstream DNS selections within NetGuardian\'s settings, providing users with greater control. I\'ve defined enum `UpstreamDnsType` with options, including local sinkhole, system default, and custom DNS resolvers. I\'m adding `customDnsIp` string value in the user preferences. The rule engine now has a check for the device to block when the device is idle.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 466588171
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
