  }
  planner_response {
    response: "I have completed the implementation of the requested features for NetGuardian, adhering to the **Sleek Interface** design aesthetic:\n\n### 1. Dedicated \'Background Activity\' Section & Execution Control\n- **Dedicated Tab Navigation**: Added a top Tab Row in the Apps screen to toggle between **App Rules** and **Background Activity**.\n- **Granular Per-App Controls**: For every installed application, users have direct toggles for:\n  - **Allow / Block Background Network**: Controls whether network sockets are permitted when the app is running in the background.\n  - **Block when Screen is OFF**: Automatically suspends socket transmissions as soon as the display turns off.\n  - **Block when Device is Idle**: Cuts socket transmissions when Android enters system Doze or device idle mode.\n- **Global Background Policy Actions**: One-tap batch controls to apply background blocking, screen-off locks, or device-idle locks to all non-system applications.\n- **Android OS & Manufacturer Limitations Transparency**: Included an expandable technical overview explaining:\n  - *Non-Root Sandboxing*: Why third-party apps cannot forcibly kill other app processes without root, and how NetGuardian\'s Linux kernel packet filtering via `VpnService` drops or sinkholes background socket traffic at the network layer.\n  - *Android Version Evolutions*: Documenting changes from Android 7.0 (Data Saver), Android 8/9 (Background Limits & App Standby Buckets), through Android 12\342\200\22314+ (Phantom Process Killer & Foreground Service restrictions).\n  - *OEM Battery Management*: Practical guidance for OEM-specific battery killers (Samsung OneUI Sleeping Apps, Xiaomi MIUI/HyperOS background restrictions, Huawei EMUI PowerGenie, and OnePlus/Oppo ColorOS Deep Optimization).\n\n### 2. DNS-Based Filtering & Local Upstream Resolver\n- **Local Domain Filtering Engine**: Evaluates outbound DNS queries locally on-device with toggles for ad blocking, tracker blocking, and malware domain blocking.\n- **Custom Blocklist Rule Management**: Lets users view, search, add, or remove custom domain rules and import/export configurations.\n- **Zero Third-Party Requirement**: Supports local loopback sinkholing (`127.0.0.1`), local network gateways, and custom user-specified DNS servers (e.g., local Pi-hole) so that no remote third-party server is mandatory.\n\n### 3. Quick Controls\n- **Comprehensive Quick Controls**: Live on the Dashboard with controls for:\n  - **Firewall ON/OFF Master Switch**\n  - **Block All Non-System Apps**\n  - **Allow All Apps**\n  - **Wi-Fi-Only Mode**\n  - **Mobile-Data-Only Mode**\n- **System Integration**: Accessible through the persistent notification actions and Android\'s Quick Settings pull-down tile (`FirewallTileService`)."
    modified_response: "I have completed the implementation of the requested features for NetGuardian, adhering to the **Sleek Interface** design aesthetic:\n\n### 1. Dedicated \'Background Activity\' Section & Execution Control\n- **Dedicated Tab Navigation**: Added a top Tab Row in the Apps screen to toggle between **App Rules** and **Background Activity**.\n- **Granular Per-App Controls**: For every installed application, users have direct toggles for:\n  - **Allow / Block Background Network**: Controls whether network sockets are permitted when the app is running in the background.\n  - **Block when Screen is OFF**: Automatically suspends socket transmissions as soon as the display turns off.\n  - **Block when Device is Idle**: Cuts socket transmissions when Android enters system Doze or device idle mode.\n- **Global Background Policy Actions**: One-tap batch controls to apply background blocking, screen-off locks, or device-idle locks to all non-system applications.\n- **Android OS & Manufacturer Limitations Transparency**: Included an expandable technical overview explaining:\n  - *Non-Root Sandboxing*: Why third-party apps cannot forcibly kill other app processes without root, and how NetGuardian\'s Linux kernel packet filtering via `VpnService` drops or sinkholes background socket traffic at the network layer.\n  - *Android Version Evolutions*: Documenting changes from Android 7.0 (Data Saver), Android 8/9 (Background Limits & App Standby Buckets), through Android 12\342\200\22314+ (Phantom Process Killer & Foreground Service restrictions).\n  - *OEM Battery Management*: Practical guidance for OEM-specific battery killers (Samsung OneUI Sleeping Apps, Xiaomi MIUI/HyperOS background restrictions, Huawei EMUI PowerGenie, and OnePlus/Oppo ColorOS Deep Optimization).\n\n### 2. DNS-Based Filtering & Local Upstream Resolver\n- **Local Domain Filtering Engine**: Evaluates outbound DNS queries locally on-device with toggles for ad blocking, tracker blocking, and malware domain blocking.\n- **Custom Blocklist Rule Management**: Lets users view, search, add, or remove custom domain rules and import/export configurations.\n- **Zero Third-Party Requirement**: Supports local loopback sinkholing (`127.0.0.1`), local network gateways, and custom user-specified DNS servers (e.g., local Pi-hole) so that no remote third-party server is mandatory.\n\n### 3. Quick Controls\n- **Comprehensive Quick Controls**: Live on the Dashboard with controls for:\n  - **Firewall ON/OFF Master Switch**\n  - **Block All Non-System Apps**\n  - **Allow All Apps**\n  - **Wi-Fi-Only Mode**\n  - **Mobile-Data-Only Mode**\n- **System Integration**: Accessible through the persistent notification actions and Android\'s Quick Settings pull-down tile (`FirewallTileService`)."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
