steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1788466590
      nanos: 681122046
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Now improve the app by implementing a **professional, foolproof first-launch onboarding and permission setup flow**.\n\nThe onboarding must be designed around the actual Android security model. Do not request permissions unnecessarily, do not show misleading permissions, and do not pretend that a permission exists when Android does not provide one.\n\n# FIRST LAUNCH EXPERIENCE\n\nWhen the user opens the app for the first time, do NOT immediately show the main dashboard.\n\nInstead launch a guided onboarding wizard:\n\n1. Welcome\n2. What the app does\n3. Privacy explanation\n4. Required setup\n5. VPN permission\n6. Notification permission\n7. Battery/background setup\n8. Optional privacy features\n9. Final verification\n10. Dashboard\n\nThe entire flow should feel polished and intentional, similar to a professional security/privacy application.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 1 \342\200\224 WELCOME\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Take control of your internet\"\n\nSubtitle:\n\n\"Control which apps can access the internet, block unwanted connections, reduce background network activity, and improve your privacy.\"\n\nShow a clean security/firewall illustration.\n\nPrimary button:\n\n\"Get Started\"\n\nSecondary option:\n\n\"Learn how it works\"\n\nDo not request any permission on this screen.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 2 \342\200\224 EXPLAIN THE APP\n\nClearly explain the three main capabilities:\n\n### Per-app Firewall\n\n\"Choose which apps can connect to the internet.\"\n\n### Background Protection\n\n\"Restrict network activity when apps are running in the background.\"\n\n### Privacy Protection\n\n\"Optionally block unwanted trackers and domains.\"\n\nAdd an important transparency notice:\n\n\"This app uses Android\'s VPN functionality to filter network traffic locally. Your traffic is not sent through our servers.\"\n\nDo not claim that the app can bypass Android restrictions.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 3 \342\200\224 PRIVACY PROMISE\n\nCreate a privacy-focused explanation:\n\n\"Your traffic stays on your device.\"\n\nShow:\n\n\342\234\223 No account required\n\342\234\223 No telemetry\n\342\234\223 No advertising SDK\n\342\234\223 No cloud server required for firewall operation\n\342\234\223 Network filtering happens locally\n\342\234\223 Firewall rules are stored locally\n\nAdd:\n\n\"Android requires a VPN permission because the firewall needs to inspect and control network traffic.\"\n\nButton:\n\n\"Continue\"\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 4 \342\200\224 SETUP CHECKLIST\n\nBefore requesting anything, show a setup checklist.\n\nExample:\n\nFirewall VPN\n\342\227\213 Not configured\n\nNotifications\n\342\227\213 Not configured\n\nBackground operation\n\342\227\213 Not configured\n\nPrivacy filters\n\342\227\213 Optional\n\nShow a progress indicator such as:\n\n\"0 of 3 required steps complete\"\n\nClearly distinguish REQUIRED from OPTIONAL items.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 5 \342\200\224 VPN PERMISSION\n\nThis is the most important setup step.\n\nExplain:\n\n\"Firewall access\"\n\n\"Android requires your approval before NetGuardian can create its local VPN connection.\"\n\nExplain clearly:\n\n\"The VPN is used to filter traffic on this device. NetGuardian does not need a remote VPN server.\"\n\nButton:\n\n\"Allow VPN Access\"\n\nWhen pressed:\n\n* Request the actual Android `VpnService` authorization.\n* Launch the official Android VPN confirmation flow.\n* Do not create a fake permission dialog.\n\nAfter returning from the system dialog:\n\n* Verify whether VPN authorization was actually granted.\n* Update the checklist.\n* If granted, show a success state.\n* If denied, show a clear explanation and allow retry.\n\nSUCCESS:\n\n\"VPN access enabled\"\n\nFAILURE:\n\n\"VPN access wasn\'t granted\"\n\nButtons:\n\n\"Try Again\"\n\n\"Continue Without Firewall\"\n\nThe second option must make it clear that the firewall will not function until VPN access is granted.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 6 \342\200\224 NOTIFICATION PERMISSION\n\nRequest notification permission only on Android versions where it is actually required.\n\nExplain why:\n\n\"Persistent firewall status\"\n\n\"Android may require notification permission so the firewall can maintain a visible foreground-service notification while protection is active.\"\n\nButton:\n\n\"Enable Notifications\"\n\nAfter the system permission dialog:\n\n* Verify the result.\n* Show success/denied state.\n* Allow retry through Android settings if appropriate.\n\nNever request notification permission if the current Android version does not require it.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 7 \342\200\224 BATTERY / BACKGROUND OPERATION\n\nCreate a dedicated explanation screen.\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Keep protection running\"\n\nExplain:\n\n\"Some Android manufacturers aggressively stop background applications. This can interrupt firewall protection.\"\n\nShow:\n\n\"Recommended\"\n\n\"Allow unrestricted battery usage\"\n\nHowever:\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo NOT silently change the user\'s battery optimization settings.\n\nInstead:\n\n* Detect whether battery optimization restrictions may affect continuous operation.\n* If Android provides an appropriate API to request exemption, launch the official system flow.\n* Clearly explain that this setting is optional and device/manufacturer dependent.\n* Never claim that battery optimization exemption guarantees perfect background operation.\n* Never use fake settings screens.\n\nButtons:\n\n\"Open Battery Settings\"\n\n\"Not Now\"\n\nIf the OS does not allow the requested control, simply explain the limitation.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 8 \342\200\224 OPTIONAL PRIVACY PROTECTION\n\nAfter required setup, introduce optional features.\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Strengthen your privacy\"\n\nOptions:\n\nTracker blocking\n[ OFF ]\n\nAd-domain blocking\n[ OFF ]\n\nMalware-domain blocking\n[ OFF ]\n\nCustom DNS/blocklists\n[ OFF ]\n\nExplain:\n\n\"These features are optional. The firewall itself does not require them.\"\n\nDo not force the user to enable DNS filtering.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 9 \342\200\224 SYSTEM ACCESS CHECK\n\nCreate a final diagnostic screen.\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Checking your setup\"\n\nRun real checks.\n\nVerify:\n\n### Firewall\n\n* VPN authorization\n* VPN service available\n* Firewall service can start\n* Local VPN interface can be established\n\n### Notifications\n\n* Required notification permission available\n\n### Background operation\n\n* Check relevant battery optimization state where APIs permit\n\n### App visibility\n\n* Installed application list can be queried\n\n### Storage\n\n* Local database is accessible\n\n### Network\n\n* Detect whether a network is currently available\n\nEach item should show:\n\n\342\234\223 Ready\n! Needs attention\n\342\200\224 Not required\n\nDo not report success unless the underlying check actually succeeds.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 10 \342\200\224 SETUP COMPLETE\n\nShow a polished completion screen.\n\nExample:\n\n\"You\'re protected\"\n\nFirewall\n\342\234\223 Ready\n\nApp controls\n\342\234\223 Ready\n\nConnection logging\n\342\234\223 Ready\n\nPrivacy filters\n\342\227\213 Optional\n\nButton:\n\n\"Open Firewall\"\n\nWhen the user enters the dashboard for the first time, the firewall should still be OFF unless the user explicitly enabled it.\n\nDo not automatically activate a network-intercepting VPN without clear user action.\n\n---\n\n# PERMISSION MANAGEMENT CENTER\n\nAdd a permanent \"Protection Setup\" / \"Permissions\" page under Settings.\n\nIt should show the current state of every capability.\n\nExample:\n\nProtection Setup\n\nVPN Firewall\n\342\234\223 Enabled\n\nNotifications\n\342\234\223 Enabled\n\nBattery Optimization\n! Restricted\n\nInstalled Apps Access\n\342\234\223 Available\n\nPrivacy Filters\n\342\227\213 Optional\n\nEach item must have:\n\n* Status\n* Description\n* Fix button where possible\n\nExample:\n\nBattery Optimization\n\"Your device may restrict background operation.\"\n\n[Fix]\n\n---\n\n# PERMISSION DENIAL HANDLING\n\nThe app must gracefully handle all cases.\n\nFor every permission:\n\nGRANTED\n\342\206\222 continue normally\n\nDENIED\n\342\206\222 explain what functionality is affected\n\nPERMANENTLY DENIED / SETTINGS REQUIRED\n\342\206\222 provide a button opening the appropriate Android Settings screen\n\nUNAVAILABLE ON DEVICE\n\342\206\222 mark the feature as unavailable instead of failing\n\nDo not repeatedly spam permission dialogs.\n\nRemember the user\'s choices.\n\n---\n\n# FIREWALL STARTUP SAFETY\n\nBefore starting the VPN firewall for the first time, show a confirmation screen:\n\n\"Start Firewall?\"\n\nExplain:\n\n\"Android will route network traffic through NetGuardian so blocked connections can be filtered.\"\n\n\"Your network traffic is processed locally according to your firewall rules.\"\n\nButtons:\n\n\"Start Firewall\"\n\n\"Cancel\"\n\nAfter the user confirms:\n\n1. Start the foreground service.\n2. Establish `VpnService`.\n3. Verify the VPN interface is actually running.\n4. Only then mark Firewall = ON.\n\nIf startup fails:\n\n* Do not show ON.\n* Show the real error.\n* Provide retry/recovery instructions.\n\n---\n\n# FIRST-TIME DEFAULT RULES\n\nDo not unexpectedly block applications immediately after onboarding.\n\nUse a safe default:\n\n\"Allow all apps\"\n\nThen let the user customize individual applications.\n\nOffer an optional quick setup:\n\n\"Want stricter defaults?\"\n\nChoices:\n\n\342\227\213 Allow all apps\n\342\227\213 Ask me per app\n\342\227\213 Block newly installed apps\n\nNever silently choose an aggressive firewall policy.\n\n---\n\n# APP ACCESS EXPLANATION\n\nWhen the user opens App Manager for the first time, explain:\n\n\"Select an app to control its network access.\"\n\nExample rule descriptions:\n\nAllow\n\"Network traffic is permitted.\"\n\nBlock\n\"Network traffic from this app is blocked while the firewall is active.\"\n\nBackground Block\n\"Attempts to restrict network communication while the app is not actively being used, subject to Android limitations.\"\n\nWi-Fi / Mobile Data controls must only be presented as effective if the implementation can actually enforce them through the firewall.\n\nDo not represent Android system-level mobile-data permission controls as available when they are not.\n\n---\n\n# ANDROID VERSION COMPATIBILITY\n\nImplement version-aware behavior.\n\nThe app must detect the Android API level and only request/use features supported by that version.\n\nFor example:\n\n* Notification runtime permission only where required\n* Battery optimization APIs only where available\n* VPN APIs according to Android version\n* Package visibility rules according to Android version\n* Foreground service requirements according to Android version\n\nUse feature detection instead of assuming one Android version.\n\n---\n\n# MANUFACTURER-SPECIFIC ROBUSTNESS\n\nMany Android manufacturers modify background behavior.\n\nThe app should detect and communicate potential restrictions without making unsupported claims.\n\nWhere practical, provide a generic:\n\n\"Background protection may be restricted by your device manufacturer.\"\n\nThen provide:\n\n\"Learn how to allow background operation\"\n\nrather than attempting undocumented hacks.\n\nDo not use Accessibility Service merely to work around normal Android restrictions.\n\nDo not use Device Administrator unless there is a legitimate feature that genuinely requires it.\n\nDo not request root.\n\nDo not request unnecessary permissions.\n\n---\n\n# ERROR RECOVERY\n\nCreate a centralized setup/diagnostics system.\n\nPossible states:\n\nVPN_NOT_AUTHORIZED\nVPN_AUTHORIZED\nVPN_START_FAILED\nVPN_RUNNING\nNOTIFICATION_NOT_GRANTED\nBATTERY_RESTRICTED\nAPP_LIST_UNAVAILABLE\nDATABASE_ERROR\nNETWORK_UNAVAILABLE\n\nEach state should have:\n\n* Human-readable explanation\n* Technical diagnostic information for logs\n* Recovery action when possible\n\n---\n\n# UI REQUIREMENTS\n\nThe onboarding should feel premium.\n\nUse Material 3 + Jetpack Compose.\n\nInclude:\n\n* Large visual hierarchy\n* Progress indicator\n* Step counter\n* Security status icons\n* Clear primary action\n* Minimal secondary actions\n* Dark/light theme\n* Smooth transitions\n* Accessible typography\n* Large touch targets\n\nDo not overwhelm the user with technical Android terminology.\n\nUse progressive disclosure: explain the technical details only when necessary.\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT IMPLEMENTATION RULE\n\nThis onboarding must be connected to REAL Android state.\n\nDo not create simulated permission states.\n\nFor every permission/status shown in the UI, query the actual Android API or actual application/service state.\n\nThe onboarding must survive:\n\n* App restart\n* Device reboot\n* Permission denial\n* Permission later revoked from Settings\n* VPN stopped externally\n* Battery restrictions changing\n* Android OS upgrade\n\nOn every application launch, re-check critical state instead of trusting only locally stored flags.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL VALIDATION\n\nBefore considering the onboarding complete, verify:\n\n1. VPN authorization is real.\n2. VPN service can actually start.\n3. Foreground service requirements are satisfied.\n4. Notification state is correct.\n5. App list is available.\n6. Firewall rules can be persisted.\n7. Firewall status reflects the actual VPN state.\n8. Permission changes made outside the app are detected.\n9. The app never claims functionality that Android does not permit.\n10. The user can always recover from a failed or denied setup step.\n\nPrioritize **real functionality, correct Android behavior, transparency, and safe defaults** over visual mockups."
    items {
      text: "Now improve the app by implementing a **professional, foolproof first-launch onboarding and permission setup flow**.\n\nThe onboarding must be designed around the actual Android security model. Do not request permissions unnecessarily, do not show misleading permissions, and do not pretend that a permission exists when Android does not provide one.\n\n# FIRST LAUNCH EXPERIENCE\n\nWhen the user opens the app for the first time, do NOT immediately show the main dashboard.\n\nInstead launch a guided onboarding wizard:\n\n1. Welcome\n2. What the app does\n3. Privacy explanation\n4. Required setup\n5. VPN permission\n6. Notification permission\n7. Battery/background setup\n8. Optional privacy features\n9. Final verification\n10. Dashboard\n\nThe entire flow should feel polished and intentional, similar to a professional security/privacy application.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 1 \342\200\224 WELCOME\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Take control of your internet\"\n\nSubtitle:\n\n\"Control which apps can access the internet, block unwanted connections, reduce background network activity, and improve your privacy.\"\n\nShow a clean security/firewall illustration.\n\nPrimary button:\n\n\"Get Started\"\n\nSecondary option:\n\n\"Learn how it works\"\n\nDo not request any permission on this screen.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 2 \342\200\224 EXPLAIN THE APP\n\nClearly explain the three main capabilities:\n\n### Per-app Firewall\n\n\"Choose which apps can connect to the internet.\"\n\n### Background Protection\n\n\"Restrict network activity when apps are running in the background.\"\n\n### Privacy Protection\n\n\"Optionally block unwanted trackers and domains.\"\n\nAdd an important transparency notice:\n\n\"This app uses Android\'s VPN functionality to filter network traffic locally. Your traffic is not sent through our servers.\"\n\nDo not claim that the app can bypass Android restrictions.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 3 \342\200\224 PRIVACY PROMISE\n\nCreate a privacy-focused explanation:\n\n\"Your traffic stays on your device.\"\n\nShow:\n\n\342\234\223 No account required\n\342\234\223 No telemetry\n\342\234\223 No advertising SDK\n\342\234\223 No cloud server required for firewall operation\n\342\234\223 Network filtering happens locally\n\342\234\223 Firewall rules are stored locally\n\nAdd:\n\n\"Android requires a VPN permission because the firewall needs to inspect and control network traffic.\"\n\nButton:\n\n\"Continue\"\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 4 \342\200\224 SETUP CHECKLIST\n\nBefore requesting anything, show a setup checklist.\n\nExample:\n\nFirewall VPN\n\342\227\213 Not configured\n\nNotifications\n\342\227\213 Not configured\n\nBackground operation\n\342\227\213 Not configured\n\nPrivacy filters\n\342\227\213 Optional\n\nShow a progress indicator such as:\n\n\"0 of 3 required steps complete\"\n\nClearly distinguish REQUIRED from OPTIONAL items.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 5 \342\200\224 VPN PERMISSION\n\nThis is the most important setup step.\n\nExplain:\n\n\"Firewall access\"\n\n\"Android requires your approval before NetGuardian can create its local VPN connection.\"\n\nExplain clearly:\n\n\"The VPN is used to filter traffic on this device. NetGuardian does not need a remote VPN server.\"\n\nButton:\n\n\"Allow VPN Access\"\n\nWhen pressed:\n\n* Request the actual Android `VpnService` authorization.\n* Launch the official Android VPN confirmation flow.\n* Do not create a fake permission dialog.\n\nAfter returning from the system dialog:\n\n* Verify whether VPN authorization was actually granted.\n* Update the checklist.\n* If granted, show a success state.\n* If denied, show a clear explanation and allow retry.\n\nSUCCESS:\n\n\"VPN access enabled\"\n\nFAILURE:\n\n\"VPN access wasn\'t granted\"\n\nButtons:\n\n\"Try Again\"\n\n\"Continue Without Firewall\"\n\nThe second option must make it clear that the firewall will not function until VPN access is granted.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 6 \342\200\224 NOTIFICATION PERMISSION\n\nRequest notification permission only on Android versions where it is actually required.\n\nExplain why:\n\n\"Persistent firewall status\"\n\n\"Android may require notification permission so the firewall can maintain a visible foreground-service notification while protection is active.\"\n\nButton:\n\n\"Enable Notifications\"\n\nAfter the system permission dialog:\n\n* Verify the result.\n* Show success/denied state.\n* Allow retry through Android settings if appropriate.\n\nNever request notification permission if the current Android version does not require it.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 7 \342\200\224 BATTERY / BACKGROUND OPERATION\n\nCreate a dedicated explanation screen.\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Keep protection running\"\n\nExplain:\n\n\"Some Android manufacturers aggressively stop background applications. This can interrupt firewall protection.\"\n\nShow:\n\n\"Recommended\"\n\n\"Allow unrestricted battery usage\"\n\nHowever:\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo NOT silently change the user\'s battery optimization settings.\n\nInstead:\n\n* Detect whether battery optimization restrictions may affect continuous operation.\n* If Android provides an appropriate API to request exemption, launch the official system flow.\n* Clearly explain that this setting is optional and device/manufacturer dependent.\n* Never claim that battery optimization exemption guarantees perfect background operation.\n* Never use fake settings screens.\n\nButtons:\n\n\"Open Battery Settings\"\n\n\"Not Now\"\n\nIf the OS does not allow the requested control, simply explain the limitation.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 8 \342\200\224 OPTIONAL PRIVACY PROTECTION\n\nAfter required setup, introduce optional features.\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Strengthen your privacy\"\n\nOptions:\n\nTracker blocking\n[ OFF ]\n\nAd-domain blocking\n[ OFF ]\n\nMalware-domain blocking\n[ OFF ]\n\nCustom DNS/blocklists\n[ OFF ]\n\nExplain:\n\n\"These features are optional. The firewall itself does not require them.\"\n\nDo not force the user to enable DNS filtering.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 9 \342\200\224 SYSTEM ACCESS CHECK\n\nCreate a final diagnostic screen.\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Checking your setup\"\n\nRun real checks.\n\nVerify:\n\n### Firewall\n\n* VPN authorization\n* VPN service available\n* Firewall service can start\n* Local VPN interface can be established\n\n### Notifications\n\n* Required notification permission available\n\n### Background operation\n\n* Check relevant battery optimization state where APIs permit\n\n### App visibility\n\n* Installed application list can be queried\n\n### Storage\n\n* Local database is accessible\n\n### Network\n\n* Detect whether a network is currently available\n\nEach item should show:\n\n\342\234\223 Ready\n! Needs attention\n\342\200\224 Not required\n\nDo not report success unless the underlying check actually succeeds.\n\n---\n\n# SCREEN 10 \342\200\224 SETUP COMPLETE\n\nShow a polished completion screen.\n\nExample:\n\n\"You\'re protected\"\n\nFirewall\n\342\234\223 Ready\n\nApp controls\n\342\234\223 Ready\n\nConnection logging\n\342\234\223 Ready\n\nPrivacy filters\n\342\227\213 Optional\n\nButton:\n\n\"Open Firewall\"\n\nWhen the user enters the dashboard for the first time, the firewall should still be OFF unless the user explicitly enabled it.\n\nDo not automatically activate a network-intercepting VPN without clear user action.\n\n---\n\n# PERMISSION MANAGEMENT CENTER\n\nAdd a permanent \"Protection Setup\" / \"Permissions\" page under Settings.\n\nIt should show the current state of every capability.\n\nExample:\n\nProtection Setup\n\nVPN Firewall\n\342\234\223 Enabled\n\nNotifications\n\342\234\223 Enabled\n\nBattery Optimization\n! Restricted\n\nInstalled Apps Access\n\342\234\223 Available\n\nPrivacy Filters\n\342\227\213 Optional\n\nEach item must have:\n\n* Status\n* Description\n* Fix button where possible\n\nExample:\n\nBattery Optimization\n\"Your device may restrict background operation.\"\n\n[Fix]\n\n---\n\n# PERMISSION DENIAL HANDLING\n\nThe app must gracefully handle all cases.\n\nFor every permission:\n\nGRANTED\n\342\206\222 continue normally\n\nDENIED\n\342\206\222 explain what functionality is affected\n\nPERMANENTLY DENIED / SETTINGS REQUIRED\n\342\206\222 provide a button opening the appropriate Android Settings screen\n\nUNAVAILABLE ON DEVICE\n\342\206\222 mark the feature as unavailable instead of failing\n\nDo not repeatedly spam permission dialogs.\n\nRemember the user\'s choices.\n\n---\n\n# FIREWALL STARTUP SAFETY\n\nBefore starting the VPN firewall for the first time, show a confirmation screen:\n\n\"Start Firewall?\"\n\nExplain:\n\n\"Android will route network traffic through NetGuardian so blocked connections can be filtered.\"\n\n\"Your network traffic is processed locally according to your firewall rules.\"\n\nButtons:\n\n\"Start Firewall\"\n\n\"Cancel\"\n\nAfter the user confirms:\n\n1. Start the foreground service.\n2. Establish `VpnService`.\n3. Verify the VPN interface is actually running.\n4. Only then mark Firewall = ON.\n\nIf startup fails:\n\n* Do not show ON.\n* Show the real error.\n* Provide retry/recovery instructions.\n\n---\n\n# FIRST-TIME DEFAULT RULES\n\nDo not unexpectedly block applications immediately after onboarding.\n\nUse a safe default:\n\n\"Allow all apps\"\n\nThen let the user customize individual applications.\n\nOffer an optional quick setup:\n\n\"Want stricter defaults?\"\n\nChoices:\n\n\342\227\213 Allow all apps\n\342\227\213 Ask me per app\n\342\227\213 Block newly installed apps\n\nNever silently choose an aggressive firewall policy.\n\n---\n\n# APP ACCESS EXPLANATION\n\nWhen the user opens App Manager for the first time, explain:\n\n\"Select an app to control its network access.\"\n\nExample rule descriptions:\n\nAllow\n\"Network traffic is permitted.\"\n\nBlock\n\"Network traffic from this app is blocked while the firewall is active.\"\n\nBackground Block\n\"Attempts to restrict network communication while the app is not actively being used, subject to Android limitations.\"\n\nWi-Fi / Mobile Data controls must only be presented as effective if the implementation can actually enforce them through the firewall.\n\nDo not represent Android system-level mobile-data permission controls as available when they are not.\n\n---\n\n# ANDROID VERSION COMPATIBILITY\n\nImplement version-aware behavior.\n\nThe app must detect the Android API level and only request/use features supported by that version.\n\nFor example:\n\n* Notification runtime permission only where required\n* Battery optimization APIs only where available\n* VPN APIs according to Android version\n* Package visibility rules according to Android version\n* Foreground service requirements according to Android version\n\nUse feature detection instead of assuming one Android version.\n\n---\n\n# MANUFACTURER-SPECIFIC ROBUSTNESS\n\nMany Android manufacturers modify background behavior.\n\nThe app should detect and communicate potential restrictions without making unsupported claims.\n\nWhere practical, provide a generic:\n\n\"Background protection may be restricted by your device manufacturer.\"\n\nThen provide:\n\n\"Learn how to allow background operation\"\n\nrather than attempting undocumented hacks.\n\nDo not use Accessibility Service merely to work around normal Android restrictions.\n\nDo not use Device Administrator unless there is a legitimate feature that genuinely requires it.\n\nDo not request root.\n\nDo not request unnecessary permissions.\n\n---\n\n# ERROR RECOVERY\n\nCreate a centralized setup/diagnostics system.\n\nPossible states:\n\nVPN_NOT_AUTHORIZED\nVPN_AUTHORIZED\nVPN_START_FAILED\nVPN_RUNNING\nNOTIFICATION_NOT_GRANTED\nBATTERY_RESTRICTED\nAPP_LIST_UNAVAILABLE\nDATABASE_ERROR\nNETWORK_UNAVAILABLE\n\nEach state should have:\n\n* Human-readable explanation\n* Technical diagnostic information for logs\n* Recovery action when possible\n\n---\n\n# UI REQUIREMENTS\n\nThe onboarding should feel premium.\n\nUse Material 3 + Jetpack Compose.\n\nInclude:\n\n* Large visual hierarchy\n* Progress indicator\n* Step counter\n* Security status icons\n* Clear primary action\n* Minimal secondary actions\n* Dark/light theme\n* Smooth transitions\n* Accessible typography\n* Large touch targets\n\nDo not overwhelm the user with technical Android terminology.\n\nUse progressive disclosure: explain the technical details only when necessary.\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT IMPLEMENTATION RULE\n\nThis onboarding must be connected to REAL Android state.\n\nDo not create simulated permission states.\n\nFor every permission/status shown in the UI, query the actual Android API or actual application/service state.\n\nThe onboarding must survive:\n\n* App restart\n* Device reboot\n* Permission denial\n* Permission later revoked from Settings\n* VPN stopped externally\n* Battery restrictions changing\n* Android OS upgrade\n\nOn every application launch, re-check critical state instead of trusting only locally stored flags.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL VALIDATION\n\nBefore considering the onboarding complete, verify:\n\n1. VPN authorization is real.\n2. VPN service can actually start.\n3. Foreground service requirements are satisfied.\n4. Notification state is correct.\n5. App list is available.\n6. Firewall rules can be persisted.\n7. Firewall status reflects the actual VPN state.\n8. Permission changes made outside the app are detected.\n9. The app never claims functionality that Android does not permit.\n10. The user can always recover from a failed or denied setup step.\n\nPrioritize **real functionality, correct Android behavior, transparency, and safe defaults** over visual mockups."
