  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing App Access Guidance**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a plan to add a first-time user guidance card, or a dismissible banner, within `AppsScreen.kt`. The message I\'m considering is: \"Select an app to control its network access,\" followed by an explanation of the Allow, Block, and Background Block options.\n\n\n**Expanding Onboarding Details**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of a comprehensive onboarding flow. I\'m imagining a ten-step guided wizard. The initial steps will introduce the app\'s capabilities, like per-app firewall and background protection. Step one should welcome the user, and explain the app\'s key benefits. I have ideas for illustrations, and I\'ll avoid requesting any permissions right away. Step two will detail the app\'s main functionalities, and include a transparency notice.\n\n\n**Refining Privacy Explanations**\n\nI\'m focusing now on steps three, four, and five of the onboarding. Step three will be a privacy promise, highlighting local processing and no cloud dependency, and it will be complete with a checklist emphasizing privacy features. Step four will present a setup checklist, clearly distinguishing required versus optional steps, with a progress indicator. Finally, step five will detail the VPN permission and explain the reasons it is required on Android.\n\n\n**Detailing VPN Permission Flow**\n\nI\'m now outlining steps six and seven, and detailing the crucial VPN permission flow. Step six involves requesting notification permissions, conditionally, only on Android 13 or later. Step seven focuses on the VPN, starting with a concise explanation: \"The VPN is used to filter traffic locally. NetGuardian doesn\'t need a remote server.\" I\'m also planning the \"Allow VPN Access\" button\'s function, detailing the Android flow with success and failure states, including \"Try Again\" and \"Continue Without Firewall\" options.\n\n\n**Elaborating Notification Setup**\n\nI\'m now detailing the Android-specific notification setup in the onboarding process. For older Android versions, I\'ll indicate that notification permission isn\'t required with an explanation regarding persistent firewall status and a foreground-service notification. I will include an \"Enable Notifications\" button that uses `ActivityResultContracts.RequestPermission()` and handle both success and denial states, including opening Android\'s settings.\n\n**Addressing Battery Optimization Setup**\n\nI am now structuring the battery and background setup step. I will include a \"Keep protection running\" section, and explain why some Android systems may shut down the firewall. I will also check to see if the system is ignoring battery optimizations, and, if not, I\'ll launch an Intent to request that the user allow it.\n\n\n**Finalizing User-Flow Steps**\n\nI\'m wrapping up steps eight and nine of the onboarding sequence. In step eight, I\'m integrating optional privacy enhancements like tracker, ad, and malware blocking, and custom DNS options, all connected to the preferences, and database. Step nine focuses on real-time system diagnostics. I\'m implementing checks for firewall authorization, notification status, battery optimization, app visibility, storage/database accessibility, and network connectivity, using real queries to ensure a functional setup.\n\n\n**Concluding Onboarding Flow**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the tenth and final step of the onboarding process, which confirms a successful setup. It will display a summary showing the firewall status, app controls, connection logging, and configured privacy filters. Users will also be able to select from safe default rules, and then open the firewall interface. I have also ensured that the firewall will start in the OFF state, requiring user action to activate the VPN. Lastly, I\'ve confirmed that completing the onboarding updates `onboardingCompleted` in `DataStore`.\n\n\n**Establishing Protection Features**\n\nI\'m now thinking about a dedicated \"Protection Setup & Permissions\" area. This will provide a concise overview of the firewall\'s crucial elements: VPN, notifications, battery optimization, app access, and privacy filters. Each will have a status display and a clear action button. I\'m also planning a confirmation dialog to ensure users understand how the firewall operates before activation.\n\n\n"
