  planner_response {
    response: "I have implemented the guided first-launch onboarding experience and permission management architecture for NetGuardian, aligned with the Android security model and Material 3 design system.\n\n### Core Enhancements\n\n- **Guided 10-Step Onboarding Wizard**:\n  - **Screen 1 \342\200\224 Welcome**: Introduces NetGuardian with a polished security badge, the title *\"Take control of your internet\"*, and transparent action options.\n  - **Screen 2 \342\200\224 Capabilities**: Explains Per-App Firewall, Background Protection, and Privacy Protection with a clear disclosure that all packet inspection occurs locally without external relay servers.\n  - **Screen 3 \342\200\224 Privacy Promise**: Highlights the zero-cloud, offline-first architecture with six distinct privacy commitments (no accounts, no telemetry, no ad SDKs, local filtering, and on-device rule storage).\n  - **Screen 4 \342\200\224 Setup Checklist**: Establishes clear expectations upfront by distinguishing required permissions (Firewall VPN, Notifications on Android 13+) from optional settings (Battery Optimization, Privacy Filters).\n  - **Screen 5 \342\200\224 Firewall VPN Access**: Integrates real `VpnService.prepare` authorization dialogs. Distinguishes granted, denied, and retry states, allowing users to proceed gracefully without an unexpected lock-in.\n  - **Screen 6 \342\200\224 Notification Permission**: Respects Android platform versioning\342\200\224requests `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` runtime permission on Android 13+ (API 33+) while automatically showing *\"Not required on this Android version\"* on older releases.\n  - **Screen 7 \342\200\224 Background Operation**: Detects battery optimization state via `PowerManager.isIgnoringBatteryOptimizations` and provides a direct shortcut to system battery settings while noting that manufacturer background policies vary.\n  - **Screen 8 \342\200\224 Optional Privacy Protection**: Provides immediate toggles for tracker blocking, ad-domain blocking, malware protection, and DNS sinkholing before entering the main workspace.\n  - **Screen 9 \342\200\224 System Access Check**: Runs real-time diagnostics across 7 system vectors (VPN Authorization, Engine Availability, Notifications, Background Execution, Installed App Visibility, Local Database Storage, and Network Connectivity) displaying **\342\234\223 Ready**, **! Needs attention**, or **\342\200\224 Not required**.\n  - **Screen 10 \342\200\224 Setup Complete & Safe Defaults**: Displays the protection overview and lets the user choose their baseline rule policy (*Allow all apps* as the safe default, or *Block newly installed apps*) before entering the dashboard with the firewall safely powered off.\n\n- **Firewall Startup Safety**:\n  - Displays a confirmation dialog before the firewall is first powered on to explain how Android routes network traffic locally.\n  - Validates service activation and provides an error recovery dialog with specific remediation steps if another VPN is already active in lockdown mode.\n\n- **Permanent Protection Setup & Permissions Center**:\n  - Integrated a dedicated **Protection Setup** card at the top of the Settings screen showing the live operational state of VPN Firewall, Notifications, Battery Optimization, App Query Access, and Privacy Filters, complete with contextual **[Fix]** and **[Settings]** actions.\n  - Listens to lifecycle resume events (`ON_RESUME`) so permission adjustments made in Android system settings update immediately when returning to the app.\n\n- **App Access Transparency**:\n  - Added an explanation card on the App Rules screen clarifying why package visibility is used, dismissible with an **[Understood]** action."
    modified_response: "I have implemented the guided first-launch onboarding experience and permission management architecture for NetGuardian, aligned with the Android security model and Material 3 design system.\n\n### Core Enhancements\n\n- **Guided 10-Step Onboarding Wizard**:\n  - **Screen 1 \342\200\224 Welcome**: Introduces NetGuardian with a polished security badge, the title *\"Take control of your internet\"*, and transparent action options.\n  - **Screen 2 \342\200\224 Capabilities**: Explains Per-App Firewall, Background Protection, and Privacy Protection with a clear disclosure that all packet inspection occurs locally without external relay servers.\n  - **Screen 3 \342\200\224 Privacy Promise**: Highlights the zero-cloud, offline-first architecture with six distinct privacy commitments (no accounts, no telemetry, no ad SDKs, local filtering, and on-device rule storage).\n  - **Screen 4 \342\200\224 Setup Checklist**: Establishes clear expectations upfront by distinguishing required permissions (Firewall VPN, Notifications on Android 13+) from optional settings (Battery Optimization, Privacy Filters).\n  - **Screen 5 \342\200\224 Firewall VPN Access**: Integrates real `VpnService.prepare` authorization dialogs. Distinguishes granted, denied, and retry states, allowing users to proceed gracefully without an unexpected lock-in.\n  - **Screen 6 \342\200\224 Notification Permission**: Respects Android platform versioning\342\200\224requests `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` runtime permission on Android 13+ (API 33+) while automatically showing *\"Not required on this Android version\"* on older releases.\n  - **Screen 7 \342\200\224 Background Operation**: Detects battery optimization state via `PowerManager.isIgnoringBatteryOptimizations` and provides a direct shortcut to system battery settings while noting that manufacturer background policies vary.\n  - **Screen 8 \342\200\224 Optional Privacy Protection**: Provides immediate toggles for tracker blocking, ad-domain blocking, malware protection, and DNS sinkholing before entering the main workspace.\n  - **Screen 9 \342\200\224 System Access Check**: Runs real-time diagnostics across 7 system vectors (VPN Authorization, Engine Availability, Notifications, Background Execution, Installed App Visibility, Local Database Storage, and Network Connectivity) displaying **\342\234\223 Ready**, **! Needs attention**, or **\342\200\224 Not required**.\n  - **Screen 10 \342\200\224 Setup Complete & Safe Defaults**: Displays the protection overview and lets the user choose their baseline rule policy (*Allow all apps* as the safe default, or *Block newly installed apps*) before entering the dashboard with the firewall safely powered off.\n\n- **Firewall Startup Safety**:\n  - Displays a confirmation dialog before the firewall is first powered on to explain how Android routes network traffic locally.\n  - Validates service activation and provides an error recovery dialog with specific remediation steps if another VPN is already active in lockdown mode.\n\n- **Permanent Protection Setup & Permissions Center**:\n  - Integrated a dedicated **Protection Setup** card at the top of the Settings screen showing the live operational state of VPN Firewall, Notifications, Battery Optimization, App Query Access, and Privacy Filters, complete with contextual **[Fix]** and **[Settings]** actions.\n  - Listens to lifecycle resume events (`ON_RESUME`) so permission adjustments made in Android system settings update immediately when returning to the app.\n\n- **App Access Transparency**:\n  - Added an explanation card on the App Rules screen clarifying why package visibility is used, dismissible with an **[Understood]** action."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1788467329
      nanos: 589994692
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Now add a **professional Android Quick Settings Tile system and persistent firewall notification** to NetGuardian.\n\n# 1. QUICK SETTINGS TILES\n\nImplement real Android `TileService` support.\n\nDo NOT create a fake tile inside the app UI. The tiles must be available from the Android system Quick Settings panel.\n\nCreate these tiles:\n\n### Tile 1 \342\200\224 Firewall\n\nLabel:\n\n\"Firewall\"\n\nStates:\n\nON\n\342\206\222 firewall is actively running\n\nOFF\n\342\206\222 firewall is stopped\n\nWhen the user taps the tile:\n\nOFF \342\206\222 start firewall\n\nON \342\206\222 stop firewall\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\n* Before starting the firewall, verify VPN authorization.\n* If VPN permission has not been granted, tapping the tile should open the appropriate app/setup screen explaining that permission is required.\n* Never claim the firewall is ON until `VpnService` is actually running.\n* If startup fails, keep the tile OFF and show the real error.\n\nThe tile must update its state when:\n\n* Firewall starts\n* Firewall stops\n* VPN disconnects\n* App process is restarted\n* Device reboots\n* User changes firewall state from inside the app\n\n---\n\n# 2. BLOCK ALL APPS TILE\n\nAdd another Quick Settings tile:\n\n\"Block All\"\n\nPurpose:\n\nInstantly apply a strict firewall mode that blocks network access for user applications.\n\nStates:\n\nACTIVE\nINACTIVE\n\nWhen enabled:\n\n* Apply the predefined \"Block all non-system/user apps\" rule.\n* Preserve the user\'s normal rules so they can be restored later.\n* Clearly distinguish this emergency/strict mode from ordinary per-app rules.\n\nWhen disabled:\n\n* Restore the previous firewall policy.\n\nDo not block critical Android/system networking required for the firewall itself unless technically necessary.\n\n---\n\n# 3. WIFI-ONLY MODE TILE\n\nAdd:\n\n\"Wi-Fi Only\"\n\nBehavior:\n\nWhen active, applications configured to use the internet should be restricted so that the firewall permits the intended Wi-Fi traffic while blocking mobile-data traffic where the VPN implementation can reliably distinguish the transport.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\nDo not pretend the VPN can always identify transport type if Android does not expose sufficient information.\n\nIf transport-aware filtering cannot be reliably implemented on a particular Android version/device, show the feature as unsupported rather than providing a fake toggle.\n\n---\n\n# 4. MOBILE-DATA BLOCK TILE\n\nAdd:\n\n\"Mobile Data\"\n\nThe tile should control the app\'s firewall policy for mobile-data traffic only where the underlying implementation can reliably enforce this.\n\nState:\n\nAllowed\nBlocked\n\nDo not claim to change Android\'s global mobile-data switch.\n\nThis is an application firewall feature, not a system mobile-data toggle.\n\n---\n\n# 5. TILE DESIGN\n\nUse standard Android `TileService`.\n\nEach tile should:\n\n* Have a meaningful icon\n* Show accurate active/inactive state\n* Update immediately\n* Use the correct Android tile lifecycle\n* Avoid unnecessary background work\n* Handle unavailable/locked states correctly\n* Work after app process death\n* Read the current firewall state from a shared local state source\n\nCreate a centralized state manager so the app UI, notification, VPN service, and Quick Settings tiles all use the same source of truth.\n\nSuggested architecture:\n\nFirewallStateRepository\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\274\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202      \342\224\202          \342\224\202\nUI   Notification  TileService\n\342\224\202                 \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200 FirewallService \342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200 VPN\n\nDo not maintain separate independent states.\n\n---\n\n# 6. TILE LABELS\n\nUse concise labels because Android Quick Settings has limited space.\n\nRecommended:\n\nFirewall\nBlock All\nWi-Fi Only\nMobile Data\n\nIf Android limits the number of tiles or the device does not support a feature, expose the available tiles gracefully.\n\n---\n\n# 7. TILE SECURITY\n\nThe tiles must not accidentally start privileged functionality without appropriate authorization.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf Firewall is OFF and the user taps it:\n\n1. Check VPN authorization.\n2. If authorized \342\206\222 start firewall.\n3. If not authorized \342\206\222 open setup/activity explaining what is required.\n\nDo not silently request permissions from a background tile service if Android does not allow that interaction.\n\n---\n\n# 8. PERSISTENT NOTIFICATION\n\nCreate a proper foreground-service notification whenever the firewall is running.\n\nExample:\n\nTitle:\n\n\"NetGuardian is protecting your device\"\n\nContent:\n\n\"Firewall active \342\200\242 7 apps blocked\"\n\nNotification should update dynamically.\n\nShow useful information such as:\n\nFirewall\nON\n\nBlocked:\n7 apps\n142 connections\n\nButtons/actions:\n\n\"Stop Firewall\"\n\"Open App\"\n\nWhere practical also include:\n\n\"Pause 10 min\"\n\nIf Android restrictions permit an action directly from the notification, make it functional.\n\n---\n\n# 9. NOTIFICATION ACTIONS\n\nImplement notification actions using proper Android notification `PendingIntent`s.\n\n### Stop Firewall\n\nStops the foreground firewall service and updates:\n\n* Notification\n* App UI\n* Quick Settings tile\n\n### Open App\n\nOpens the main dashboard.\n\n### Pause\n\nTemporarily pauses firewall protection.\n\nAllow:\n\n5 minutes\n15 minutes\n30 minutes\n1 hour\n\nWhen the pause expires:\n\n* Automatically restore firewall protection\n* Update notification\n* Update tile state\n\nDo not silently resume if the service can no longer establish the VPN. Show the correct error state.\n\n---\n\n# 10. NOTIFICATION CONTENT\n\nThe notification should never claim protection when the VPN is not actually active.\n\nPossible states:\n\n### Firewall active\n\n\"NetGuardian is protecting your device\"\n\n### Firewall stopped\n\nDo not keep the foreground-service protection notification active.\n\n### VPN disconnected unexpectedly\n\n\"Firewall protection stopped\"\n\nAction:\n\n\"Restore Protection\"\n\n### Error\n\n\"Firewall needs attention\"\n\nAction:\n\n\"Open NetGuardian\"\n\n---\n\n# 11. NOTIFICATION CHANNELS\n\nCreate appropriate Android notification channels.\n\nExample:\n\nNetGuardian Protection\n\342\206\222 high importance only when genuinely necessary\n\nNetGuardian Status\n\342\206\222 lower importance\n\nNetGuardian Alerts\n\342\206\222 only for important firewall failures/security events\n\nDo not abuse notification importance.\n\nRespect the user\'s notification settings.\n\n---\n\n# 12. NOTIFICATION PERMISSION\n\nOn Android versions requiring runtime notification permission:\n\n* Request it during onboarding as previously specified.\n* Explain why it is needed.\n* Never assume it is granted.\n\nIf notification permission is denied:\n\n* The firewall should handle the situation according to Android foreground-service requirements.\n* Explain any functionality that may be affected.\n* Provide a Settings button to restore notification permission.\n\nDo not create fake notifications.\n\n---\n\n# 13. LOCK SCREEN BEHAVIOR\n\nMake notification visibility compatible with Android lock-screen privacy settings.\n\nDo not expose sensitive connection data unnecessarily.\n\nUse notification visibility APIs appropriately.\n\nExample:\n\nPublic:\n\n\"NetGuardian is active\"\n\nPrivate:\n\n\"7 apps blocked \342\200\242 142 connections\"\n\n---\n\n# 14. BOOT BEHAVIOR\n\nSupport reboot recovery.\n\nAfter device reboot:\n\n* Restore the user\'s configured firewall preference.\n* Do not assume the VPN is already running.\n* Start protection only according to Android\'s allowed background/service behavior and the user\'s explicit setting.\n* Verify VPN establishment.\n* Update notification and Quick Settings tiles only after the state is confirmed.\n\nIf Android prevents automatic startup, show:\n\n\"Protection needs to be restored\"\n\nand provide an actionable notification or app action.\n\n---\n\n# 15. SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH\n\nCreate a central `FirewallStateRepository`.\n\nPossible states:\n\nSTOPPED\nSTARTING\nRUNNING\nPAUSED\nERROR\nVPN_PERMISSION_REQUIRED\n\nExpose observable state to:\n\n* Compose UI\n* TileService\n* Notification manager\n* Firewall foreground service\n* VPN service\n* Setup/diagnostics screen\n\nPersist only the necessary configuration in DataStore/Room.\n\nNever use the UI\'s local state as the authoritative firewall state.\n\n---\n\n# 16. LIVE UPDATES\n\nWhen firewall state changes, immediately synchronize:\n\nAndroid Quick Settings tiles\n\342\206\223\nNotification\n\342\206\223\nDashboard\n\342\206\223\nApp list status\n\342\206\223\nConnection statistics\n\nFor example:\n\nUser taps Quick Settings \"Firewall\"\n\n\342\206\222 VPN starts\n\342\206\222 repository becomes RUNNING\n\342\206\222 tile becomes active\n\342\206\222 notification appears\n\342\206\222 dashboard shows ON\n\nIf VPN fails:\n\n\342\206\222 repository becomes ERROR\n\342\206\222 tile becomes inactive\n\342\206\222 notification shows failure\n\342\206\222 dashboard shows error\n\n---\n\n# 17. LONG-PRESS TILE BEHAVIOR\n\nWhere supported, configure appropriate tile behavior so long-pressing/opening the tile takes the user to the relevant NetGuardian screen.\n\nExamples:\n\nFirewall tile\n\342\206\222 Firewall dashboard\n\nBlock All\n\342\206\222 Strict Mode settings\n\nWi-Fi Only\n\342\206\222 Network policy settings\n\n---\n\n# 18. FIRST-TIME TILE EXPERIENCE\n\nAfter onboarding is complete, show a subtle suggestion:\n\n\"Quick access\"\n\n\"Add NetGuardian to Quick Settings for one-tap protection.\"\n\nDo not automatically manipulate the user\'s Quick Settings layout if Android does not permit it.\n\nWhere Android provides a supported mechanism to request/add a tile, use it. Otherwise give clear instructions.\n\n---\n\n# 19. BATTERY AND PERFORMANCE\n\nQuick Settings and notification functionality must be lightweight.\n\nDo not:\n\n* Poll constantly\n* Start unnecessary services\n* Maintain multiple background loops\n* Wake the device unnecessarily\n\nUse event-driven updates wherever possible.\n\nThe firewall\'s foreground service remains the primary long-running component while protection is active.\n\n---\n\n# 20. IMPLEMENTATION QUALITY\n\nUse real Android APIs:\n\n* `TileService`\n* `VpnService`\n* Foreground Service APIs\n* `NotificationManager`\n* `NotificationChannel`\n* `PendingIntent`\n* `DataStore`\n* Android lifecycle APIs\n\nUse Android API-level checks where behavior differs.\n\nDo not generate pseudo-code that cannot compile.\n\nMake the Quick Settings tiles and notification work with the actual firewall engine rather than mock state.\n\nThe final result should behave like a polished real Android firewall application."
