    thinking: "**Analyzing Core Requirements**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the requirements. The key focus is dissecting the central demands for this project, particularly the need for four distinct, real Android TileServices. I\'m aiming for a solution that\'s both efficient and solid. I\'m focusing on the first central requirement.\n\n\n**Defining Tile Services**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got the first two TileServices mapped out in `AndroidManifest.xml`, implementing them with `TileService`. \"FirewallTileService\" controls the firewall status, linked to VPN authorization. \"BlockAllTileService\" is for a strict firewall mode. I\'m focusing on their respective labels, icons, states, and the exact tap behavior for both.\n\n\n**Defining WiFi Behavior**\n\nI\'m now outlining \"WifiOnlyTileService\". It restricts internet access to Wi-Fi. I\'m focusing on states (`STATE_ACTIVE`) and tap behavior. If active, Wi-Fi traffic is permitted, while cellular is blocked. I\'m also ensuring the service handles devices without cellular capabilities or unreliable transport detection, gracefully showing its state.\n\n\n**Clarifying States & Rules**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the state definitions, considering `STATE_INACTIVE` or `STATE_UNAVAILABLE` for cellular-lacking devices. Next, I\'m working on \"MobileDataBlockTileService\", aiming for clear labeling (\"Mobile Data\" title, \"Allowed\"/\"Blocked\" subtitiles) and states: `STATE_ACTIVE` implies mobile data is blocked. I\'ll include the clear disclosure that this is an app feature, not a system toggle.\n\n\n\n**Implementing State Changes**\n\nI\'m now detailing how state changes in `FirewallStateRepository` trigger updates. I\'m focusing on the cascade: notification updates via `FirewallNotificationManager`, the `TileService.requestListeningState` calls for all tile classes (ensuring immediate QS panel updates), and state emission to the UI for live dashboard updates. I\'m also addressing the persistent notification for when the firewall is running, detailing the content and actionable buttons.\n\n\n**Designing Protection Actions**\n\nI\'m now detailing a \"Pause Protection\" feature within the dashboard. This allows temporary firewall suspension, with durations of 5 to 30 minutes, or a set \"Pause\" action. When paused, the VPN is bypassed. This will use a Coroutine or AlarmManager for scheduling a return to active protection, updating the notification and tiles.\n\nI\'m also outlining the notifications for unexpected VPN disconnects and other errors. The disconnect notification will offer a \"Restore Protection\" action, and the error notification will prompt the user to \"Open NetGuardian\". I\'ve created the notification channels I\'ll need. I\'m focusing on the importance to use of various channels.\n\nFinally, I\'m working on lock screen visibility. I intend to use the `VISIBILITY_PRIVATE` setting for detailed app/connection counts, and a public version showing \"NetGuardian is active\" or \"Device protected,\" hiding those private details.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 56232180
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
