  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Firewall State Repository**\n\nI\'m now focusing on creating the `FirewallStateRepository`. The plan is to establish it as the Single Source of Truth, as requested. I\'ve begun to define the possible firewall states: `STOPPED`, `STARTING`, `RUNNING`, `PAUSED`, and `ERROR`. These states will guide the repository\'s design.\n\n\n**Expanding State Representation**\n\nI\'ve expanded the `FirewallState` enum to include `VPN_PERMISSION_REQUIRED`. I\'m now focusing on exposing the firewall\'s observable state through `StateFlow<FirewallSnapshot>`. The plan is to broadcast this to UI elements, TileService, the Notification Manager, and other essential components. The repository will manage state transitions using methods. The data class `FirewallSnapshot` includes state, block states, and network information. I\'m focusing on the firewall configuration persistence.\n\n\n**Defining State Transitions**\n\nI\'m now outlining methods for state transitions within the `FirewallStateRepository`. I\'m defining specific functions like `setStarting()`, `setRunning()`, `setStopped()`, `setPaused()`, and others to control state changes. I\'m also considering how to update statistics, network information, and block modes using dedicated functions to ensure a clean state management approach.\n\nI\'ve also started thinking about automatic synchronization. When the snapshot changes, I\'ll update the `FirewallNotificationManager`. I plan to call `requestListeningState` for all the Quick Settings tiles. Lastly, I\'m working to persist critical state data using `PreferencesRepository` or DataStore.\n\n\n**Implementing Pause Functionality**\n\nI\'m now integrating a pause feature with configurable durations. When paused, the state will be `PAUSED`, and I\'ll adjust VPN traffic to either bypass filtering or pause it entirely, depending on the implementation. A notification will display the remaining pause time and offer a \"Resume Now\" action. A coroutine/timer will manage the countdown. Upon expiration, `resumeProtection()` restores VPN filtering and switches the state back to `RUNNING`.\n\n\n**Authorizing VPN Access**\n\nI\'m now implementing VPN authorization. I\'ll use `VpnService.prepare(this)` to check for authorization. If authorized, I\'ll start `FirewallVpnService`. If not, I\'ll direct the user to `MainActivity` or a setup screen using `startActivityAndCollapse` to request permission. I also defined an action for long-press / preferences to open `MainActivity` to the dashboard.\n\nI am now focusing on the Block All Apps Quick Settings tile, named `BlockAllTileService`. I\'ve defined its label, subtitle based on state, and click behavior to toggle strict mode, preserving previous rules, reloading VPN rules when active, and optionally prompting to start the firewall.\n\n\n**Designing Network Control Tiles**\n\nI\'m now implementing two new Quick Settings tiles: \"Wi-Fi Only\" and \"Mobile Data Block.\" The Wi-Fi tile will let the user block all cellular networks, and its state will be active, inactive or unavailable based on network availability. The mobile data block tile is also in the works. I\'m checking for telephony hardware support for the Wi-Fi tile, marking it unavailable if cellular is unsupported. Both tiles will affect rule evaluations and VPN service notifications. I\'m focusing on their respective labels, states and tap (onClick) behavior.\n\n\n**Defining Mobile Data Tile**\n\nI\'m now implementing the \"Mobile Data\" Quick Settings tile. The subtitle will indicate if mobile data is blocked, allowed, or unsupported, with an appropriate tile state. The user will have clear transparency, and a tap will toggle the policy, re-evaluating rules and updating the VPN service. I will handle the \"no cellular\" scenario correctly. I\'m focusing on icons, vector drawables for the tiles.\n\nI\'m checking Section 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13 for Persistent Notifications and Notification Channels. The persistent notification, shown during foreground service, will show details of the app.\n\n\n**Designing Notification Actions**\n\nI\'m now outlining notification actions for firewall control. I\'m defining PendingIntents for \"Stop Firewall,\" \"Open App,\" and \"Pause 10 min.\" Paused state notifications will show \"Firewall protection paused\" with a timer, offering \"Resume Protection\" and \"Open App\" actions. I\'m also planning notifications for unexpected disconnects (\"Firewall protection stopped\") with a \"Restore Protection\" action, and for errors (\"Firewall needs attention\") linking to the main app. I am checking Section 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13 for Persistent Notifications and Notification Channels.\n\n\n**Defining Notification Channels**\n\nI\'m now establishing notification channels to control user experience. I\'ve defined channels such as \"NetGuardian Protection\" with low importance for updates, a \"NetGuardian Status\" channel for pause notices, and \"NetGuardian Alerts\" for critical events. I\'m setting the lock screen visibility of the main notification to private, showing only a generic \"NetGuardian is active\" message with low priority, ensuring no private data leaks. I am now checking Section 14 for boot behavior.\n\n\\n\\n\nI\'ve implemented a `BootReceiver` that starts on boot completion. It checks `startOnBoot` in `UserPreferences` and VPN authorization. If authorized, it starts the `FirewallVpnService` automatically.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 168572197
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
