  planner_response {
    response: "The Quick Settings Tiles system and persistent firewall notification suite have been fully implemented, verified, and tested:\n\n### 1. Android Quick Settings Tile System\n- **Native `TileService` Implementation**: Built 4 distinct system tiles adhering strictly to Android\'s system Quick Settings model:\n  - **Firewall Tile (`FirewallTileService`)**: Toggles protection on/off with one-time VPN permission validation. Reflects active/inactive/unavailable states accurately.\n  - **Block All Tile (`BlockAllTileService`)**: Emergency lockdown mode that temporarily restricts non-system user applications without destroying user-configured rules.\n  - **Wi-Fi Only Tile (`WifiOnlyTileService`)**: Enforces firewall policies restricting background data to Wi-Fi networks (marks as unavailable if the device lacks cellular hardware).\n  - **Mobile Data Tile (`MobileDataTileService`)**: Provides an application-level firewall cellular restriction switch clearly distinguished from the global Android SIM radio switch.\n- **System Registration & Vector Drawables**: Registered with appropriate permissions (`BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE`) and custom monochrome vector drawables in `AndroidManifest.xml`.\n- **Permission & Lock-Screen Awareness**: Prompts the user to authorize the VPN service profile if tapped prior to granting permissions, and checks device unlock state where required.\n\n### 2. Persistent Firewall Notification\n- **`FirewallNotificationManager`**: Manages dedicated notification channels (`firewall_status_channel` and `firewall_alerts_channel`).\n- **Live Status & Real-Time Metrics**: Displays accurate status badges (`RUNNING`, `PAUSED`, `STARTING`, `ERROR`), current blocked application counts, and daily blocked connection metrics.\n- **Interactive Controls**: Includes inline action buttons to **Pause Protection** (for 10 minutes), **Resume Protection**, or **Stop Firewall**.\n- **Deep Linking**: Tapping the notification opens the dashboard or requested destination screen via typed navigation routing.\n\n### 3. Architecture & Single Source of Truth\n- **`FirewallStateRepository`**: Central singleton state holder syncing `FirewallSnapshot` with Compose UI, the persistent notification, and all 4 Quick Settings tiles simultaneously.\n- **Boot & Background Safety**: Permission-aware `BootReceiver` automatically restores protection upon device restart only if VPN authorization was previously established.\n- **Discoverability**: Integrated an in-app Quick Settings guide in `Settings` with Android 13+ (`StatusBarManager.requestAddTileService`) one-tap prompt support and step-by-step manual setup instructions."
    modified_response: "The Quick Settings Tiles system and persistent firewall notification suite have been fully implemented, verified, and tested:\n\n### 1. Android Quick Settings Tile System\n- **Native `TileService` Implementation**: Built 4 distinct system tiles adhering strictly to Android\'s system Quick Settings model:\n  - **Firewall Tile (`FirewallTileService`)**: Toggles protection on/off with one-time VPN permission validation. Reflects active/inactive/unavailable states accurately.\n  - **Block All Tile (`BlockAllTileService`)**: Emergency lockdown mode that temporarily restricts non-system user applications without destroying user-configured rules.\n  - **Wi-Fi Only Tile (`WifiOnlyTileService`)**: Enforces firewall policies restricting background data to Wi-Fi networks (marks as unavailable if the device lacks cellular hardware).\n  - **Mobile Data Tile (`MobileDataTileService`)**: Provides an application-level firewall cellular restriction switch clearly distinguished from the global Android SIM radio switch.\n- **System Registration & Vector Drawables**: Registered with appropriate permissions (`BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE`) and custom monochrome vector drawables in `AndroidManifest.xml`.\n- **Permission & Lock-Screen Awareness**: Prompts the user to authorize the VPN service profile if tapped prior to granting permissions, and checks device unlock state where required.\n\n### 2. Persistent Firewall Notification\n- **`FirewallNotificationManager`**: Manages dedicated notification channels (`firewall_status_channel` and `firewall_alerts_channel`).\n- **Live Status & Real-Time Metrics**: Displays accurate status badges (`RUNNING`, `PAUSED`, `STARTING`, `ERROR`), current blocked application counts, and daily blocked connection metrics.\n- **Interactive Controls**: Includes inline action buttons to **Pause Protection** (for 10 minutes), **Resume Protection**, or **Stop Firewall**.\n- **Deep Linking**: Tapping the notification opens the dashboard or requested destination screen via typed navigation routing.\n\n### 3. Architecture & Single Source of Truth\n- **`FirewallStateRepository`**: Central singleton state holder syncing `FirewallSnapshot` with Compose UI, the persistent notification, and all 4 Quick Settings tiles simultaneously.\n- **Boot & Background Safety**: Permission-aware `BootReceiver` automatically restores protection upon device restart only if VPN authorization was previously established.\n- **Discoverability**: Integrated an in-app Quick Settings guide in `Settings` with Android 13+ (`StatusBarManager.requestAddTileService`) one-tap prompt support and step-by-step manual setup instructions."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1788468222
      nanos: 192058069
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Now perform a **complete dark-mode UI audit and redesign** for NetGuardian.\n\nLight mode is already acceptable. **Do not significantly change the existing light-mode appearance.**\n\nThe current dark mode has incorrect text colors, weak contrast, poorly colored controls, and some UI elements that visually disappear or look inconsistent.\n\nYour task is to make the **entire application dark mode polished, readable, accessible, and visually consistent**.\n\n# 1. DARK MODE DESIGN SYSTEM\n\nCreate a centralized Material 3 dark color system.\n\nDo NOT fix colors individually with random hardcoded values.\n\nCreate a single source of truth for:\n\n* Background\n* Surface\n* Surface Variant\n* Elevated Surface\n* Primary\n* Primary Container\n* Secondary\n* Secondary Container\n* Tertiary\n* Error\n* Warning\n* Success\n* On Background\n* On Surface\n* On Surface Variant\n* On Primary\n* On Secondary\n* On Error\n* Outline\n* Outline Variant\n* Divider\n\nEvery screen and component must use the centralized theme.\n\nAvoid hardcoded colors such as:\n\nColor.White\nColor.Black\nColor.Gray\n\nunless there is a specific semantic reason.\n\nUse theme-aware Material colors instead.\n\n---\n\n# 2. DARK MODE COLORS\n\nUse a sophisticated dark UI rather than simply inverting the light theme.\n\nRecommended foundation:\n\nBackground:\nVery dark neutral / slightly cool charcoal\n\nSurface:\nDark charcoal\n\nSurface Variant:\nSlightly lighter charcoal\n\nElevated Surface:\nClearly distinguishable from the background without looking bright\n\nPrimary:\nA highly readable accent color\n\nText hierarchy:\n\nPrimary text\n\342\206\222 near-white, not pure white everywhere\n\nSecondary text\n\342\206\222 clearly visible muted light tone\n\nTertiary/supporting text\n\342\206\222 still readable, but visually subordinate\n\nDisabled text\n\342\206\222 visibly disabled but not completely invisible\n\nDo not use dark gray text on dark backgrounds.\n\nDo not use low-contrast gray icons.\n\n---\n\n# 3. CONTRAST\n\nAudit every visible component in dark mode.\n\nCheck:\n\n* Headings\n* Body text\n* Labels\n* Captions\n* Icons\n* Switches\n* Checkboxes\n* Radio buttons\n* Cards\n* Buttons\n* Navigation bars\n* Top app bars\n* Bottom navigation\n* Dialogs\n* Bottom sheets\n* Chips\n* Badges\n* Dividers\n* Progress indicators\n* Charts\n* Empty states\n* Error messages\n* Success messages\n* Warning messages\n* Connection status\n* Firewall status\n* App list\n* Quick action controls\n\nNo important information should depend on barely visible gray-on-gray styling.\n\nTarget WCAG-compatible contrast wherever practical.\n\nPrimary text should have strong contrast.\n\nSecondary text should remain comfortably readable.\n\nInteractive controls must be clearly distinguishable from their background.\n\n---\n\n# 4. TEXT COLOR HIERARCHY\n\nCreate a consistent hierarchy.\n\nExample:\n\nHeadline\n\342\206\222 highest emphasis\n\nTitle\n\342\206\222 high emphasis\n\nBody\n\342\206\222 normal emphasis\n\nSecondary information\n\342\206\222 medium emphasis\n\nMetadata\n\342\206\222 lower emphasis\n\nDisabled\n\342\206\222 lowest emphasis\n\nDo not make all text the same brightness.\n\nDo not make secondary text so dark that users cannot read it.\n\n---\n\n# 5. CARDS\n\nReview all cards throughout the app.\n\nIn dark mode:\n\n* Cards must be distinguishable from the page background.\n* Card borders should be subtle.\n* Shadows should not be the primary method of separation.\n* Text must remain readable.\n* Icons must have sufficient contrast.\n* Status indicators must remain visible.\n\nAvoid:\n\nDark background + slightly darker card + dark text.\n\nThat combination makes content unreadable.\n\n---\n\n# 6. BUTTONS\n\nAudit every button.\n\n### Filled buttons\n\nUse a strong primary/container color with correctly contrasting text.\n\n### Outlined buttons\n\nUse visible outlines and readable text.\n\n### Tonal buttons\n\nUse a clearly distinguishable surface.\n\n### Text buttons\n\nEnsure text is not too dark or muted.\n\n### Disabled buttons\n\nMake the disabled state obvious without making the button disappear.\n\nDo not use the same dark surface for both the button and the background.\n\n---\n\n# 7. SWITCHES / TOGGLES\n\nThis is especially important.\n\nAudit every switch.\n\nFor each switch make sure:\n\nON:\n\n* Track is clearly visible.\n* Thumb is clearly visible.\n* Active color is obvious.\n\nOFF:\n\n* Track remains visible.\n* Thumb remains visible.\n* The control does not visually disappear.\n\nDisabled:\n\n* Clearly communicates disabled state.\n\nDo not rely only on color.\n\nThe surrounding label/status should also communicate the state.\n\n---\n\n# 8. FIREWALL STATUS\n\nThe Firewall ON/OFF status is one of the most important elements.\n\nDark mode should make it immediately understandable.\n\nExample:\n\nFirewall ON\n\342\227\217 Active\n\nFirewall OFF\n\342\227\217 Stopped\n\nFirewall Error\n\342\227\217 Needs attention\n\nUse semantic colors carefully:\n\nSuccess\n\342\206\222 active/healthy\n\nError\n\342\206\222 failed/problem\n\nWarning\n\342\206\222 attention required\n\nDo not use overly saturated colors that damage readability.\n\n---\n\n# 9. APP LIST\n\nAudit the installed-app screen carefully.\n\nEach application row should have:\n\n* Clearly visible app name\n* Readable package/details text\n* Visible app icon\n* Clearly visible network status\n* Readable rule summary\n* Proper switch/toggle colors\n* Clear selected/expanded state\n\nExample:\n\nYouTube\nNetwork access\n\nWi-Fi     ON\nMobile    OFF\nBackground OFF\n\nEverything must remain readable in dark mode.\n\n---\n\n# 10. CONNECTION LOG\n\nImprove the dark-mode connection log.\n\nExample:\n\nYouTube\ngooglevideo.com\n443 \342\200\242 TCP\n\nALLOWED\n\nor\n\nBLOCKED\n\nMake sure:\n\n* Destination text is readable.\n* Timestamp is readable.\n* Port/protocol is readable.\n* Allowed/blocked status is highly distinguishable.\n* Long destinations don\'t become visually confusing.\n\nAvoid extremely dark gray metadata.\n\n---\n\n# 11. NAVIGATION\n\nAudit:\n\n* Top app bar\n* Bottom navigation\n* Navigation drawer if present\n* Selected icon\n* Unselected icon\n* Labels\n* Back button\n* Overflow menus\n\nSelected and unselected states must be distinguishable.\n\nUnselected icons should still be clearly visible.\n\nDo not make inactive navigation icons nearly black.\n\n---\n\n# 12. DIALOGS / BOTTOM SHEETS\n\nDark-mode dialogs and bottom sheets must have:\n\n* Appropriate elevated surface\n* Readable title\n* Readable body\n* Clearly visible buttons\n* Correct divider/outline\n* Proper scrim\n* Correct icon colors\n\nAvoid dialogs that become a black rectangle containing dark-gray text.\n\n---\n\n# 13. INPUT FIELDS\n\nAudit every text field.\n\nDark mode should correctly style:\n\n* Background\n* Label\n* Input text\n* Placeholder\n* Cursor\n* Focus indicator\n* Outline\n* Error state\n* Supporting text\n\nFocused fields must be visually obvious.\n\nError text must be readable.\n\nPlaceholder text should be muted but still accessible.\n\n---\n\n# 14. CHIPS / BADGES\n\nAudit all chips and badges.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"Blocked\"\n\"Allowed\"\n\"VPN Active\"\n\"Background\"\n\"Wi-Fi\"\n\"Mobile Data\"\n\nEnsure text and icons have sufficient contrast against their container.\n\n---\n\n# 15. EMPTY STATES\n\nImprove empty screens.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"No blocked apps\"\n\"No recent connections\"\n\"No firewall rules\"\n\nMake sure:\n\n* Illustration/icon is visible\n* Heading is readable\n* Explanation is readable\n* Action button is visible\n\nDo not use low-contrast illustrations.\n\n---\n\n# 16. ERROR / WARNING / SUCCESS STATES\n\nUse semantic colors consistently.\n\nCreate centralized semantic colors such as:\n\nSuccess\nWarning\nError\nInfo\n\nThen use those colors across the application.\n\nDo not choose slightly different green/red/yellow colors on every screen.\n\nThe same state should look the same everywhere.\n\n---\n\n# 17. CHARTS / STATISTICS\n\nAudit charts in dark mode.\n\nMake sure:\n\n* Axes are visible\n* Labels are visible\n* Grid lines are subtle but visible\n* Data lines/bars are distinguishable\n* Legends are readable\n* Tooltips are readable\n\nDo not use chart colors that disappear against the dark surface.\n\n---\n\n# 18. ICONS\n\nAudit every icon.\n\nIcons must use theme-aware colors.\n\nDo not use black icons on dark surfaces.\n\nDo not use white icons everywhere regardless of semantic meaning.\n\nUse:\n\nPrimary icon\nSecondary icon\nDisabled icon\nSuccess icon\nWarning icon\nError icon\n\nconsistently.\n\n---\n\n# 19. DIVIDERS AND BORDERS\n\nDividers must be subtle but visible.\n\nThey should not disappear completely.\n\nUse theme `outlineVariant` or an equivalent semantic color rather than arbitrary gray values.\n\n---\n\n# 20. STATUS BAR / SYSTEM UI\n\nMake dark mode work correctly with Android system UI.\n\nConfigure:\n\n* Status bar\n* Navigation bar\n* System bar icons\n* Edge-to-edge behavior\n\nEnsure system icons remain readable.\n\nDo not use a light status-bar icon configuration on a light surface or the opposite.\n\nRespect Android\'s edge-to-edge requirements.\n\n---\n\n# 21. DYNAMIC COLOR\n\nIf dynamic color is supported, integrate it carefully.\n\nDo not let dynamic colors destroy contrast.\n\nThe NetGuardian semantic colors must remain readable even with different device color palettes.\n\nProvide sensible fallbacks.\n\n---\n\n# 22. THEME ARCHITECTURE\n\nRefactor the theme so that light and dark mode share the same semantic component definitions.\n\nFor example:\n\nNetGuardianTheme\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 LightColorScheme\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 DarkColorScheme\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Typography\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Shapes\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Component styling\n\nComponents should consume semantic theme colors instead of deciding their own colors.\n\n---\n\n# 23. NO HARDCODED DARK-MODE FIXES\n\nSearch the entire project for problematic patterns such as:\n\nColor.Black\nColor.White\nColor.Gray\nColor.DarkGray\n\"#000000\"\n\"#FFFFFF\"\n\nand similar hardcoded colors.\n\nReplace them with theme-aware or semantic colors wherever appropriate.\n\nDo not blindly replace colors that have legitimate branding/illustration purposes.\n\n---\n\n# 24. AUTOMATIC DARK-MODE TESTING\n\nCreate a reusable preview/testing approach so every major component can be evaluated in:\n\nLight mode\nDark mode\n\nTest at minimum:\n\n* Home\n* Apps\n* App details\n* Connections\n* Privacy\n* Settings\n* Onboarding\n* Permission screens\n* Diagnostics\n* Dialogs\n* Bottom sheets\n* Error states\n* Empty states\n\nEnsure text is readable in every state.\n\n---\n\n# 25. ACCESSIBILITY\n\nImprove dark mode accessibility.\n\nSupport:\n\n* Large font sizes\n* Font scaling\n* Content descriptions\n* Touch targets\n* Screen readers\n* Sufficient contrast\n* State communication beyond color alone\n\nDo not communicate \"blocked\", \"allowed\", or \"error\" only through color.\n\nInclude text/icon/state indicators.\n\n---\n\n# 26. VISUAL QUALITY STANDARD\n\nThe final dark mode should feel like a polished premium Android security/privacy application.\n\nVisual direction:\n\n* Clean\n* Modern\n* Professional\n* Calm\n* High readability\n* Strong hierarchy\n* Subtle elevation\n* Consistent spacing\n* Minimal visual noise\n\nAvoid:\n\n* Pure black everywhere\n* Washed-out gray text\n* Random accent colors\n* Excessive glow\n* Excessive borders\n* Low-contrast controls\n* Bright white backgrounds inside dark mode\n* Inconsistent component styling\n\n---\n\n# 27. IMPORTANT\n\nDo NOT redesign the application\'s functionality.\n\nDo NOT remove existing features.\n\nDo NOT significantly alter the light theme.\n\nFocus on:\n\n**Dark-mode correctness + contrast + consistency + accessibility + professional visual quality.**\n\nAfter implementing the new dark theme, perform a full application-wide UI audit and fix every component that still has poor contrast or incorrect colors.\n\nThe final result must have **zero obvious dark-mode readability problems** across the entire application."
    items {
      text: "Now perform a **complete dark-mode UI audit and redesign** for NetGuardian.\n\nLight mode is already acceptable. **Do not significantly change the existing light-mode appearance.**\n\nThe current dark mode has incorrect text colors, weak contrast, poorly colored controls, and some UI elements that visually disappear or look inconsistent.\n\nYour task is to make the **entire application dark mode polished, readable, accessible, and visually consistent**.\n\n# 1. DARK MODE DESIGN SYSTEM\n\nCreate a centralized Material 3 dark color system.\n\nDo NOT fix colors individually with random hardcoded values.\n\nCreate a single source of truth for:\n\n* Background\n* Surface\n* Surface Variant\n* Elevated Surface\n* Primary\n* Primary Container\n* Secondary\n* Secondary Container\n* Tertiary\n* Error\n* Warning\n* Success\n* On Background\n* On Surface\n* On Surface Variant\n* On Primary\n* On Secondary\n* On Error\n* Outline\n* Outline Variant\n* Divider\n\nEvery screen and component must use the centralized theme.\n\nAvoid hardcoded colors such as:\n\nColor.White\nColor.Black\nColor.Gray\n\nunless there is a specific semantic reason.\n\nUse theme-aware Material colors instead.\n\n---\n\n# 2. DARK MODE COLORS\n\nUse a sophisticated dark UI rather than simply inverting the light theme.\n\nRecommended foundation:\n\nBackground:\nVery dark neutral / slightly cool charcoal\n\nSurface:\nDark charcoal\n\nSurface Variant:\nSlightly lighter charcoal\n\nElevated Surface:\nClearly distinguishable from the background without looking bright\n\nPrimary:\nA highly readable accent color\n\nText hierarchy:\n\nPrimary text\n\342\206\222 near-white, not pure white everywhere\n\nSecondary text\n\342\206\222 clearly visible muted light tone\n\nTertiary/supporting text\n\342\206\222 still readable, but visually subordinate\n\nDisabled text\n\342\206\222 visibly disabled but not completely invisible\n\nDo not use dark gray text on dark backgrounds.\n\nDo not use low-contrast gray icons.\n\n---\n\n# 3. CONTRAST\n\nAudit every visible component in dark mode.\n\nCheck:\n\n* Headings\n* Body text\n* Labels\n* Captions\n* Icons\n* Switches\n* Checkboxes\n* Radio buttons\n* Cards\n* Buttons\n* Navigation bars\n* Top app bars\n* Bottom navigation\n* Dialogs\n* Bottom sheets\n* Chips\n* Badges\n* Dividers\n* Progress indicators\n* Charts\n* Empty states\n* Error messages\n* Success messages\n* Warning messages\n* Connection status\n* Firewall status\n* App list\n* Quick action controls\n\nNo important information should depend on barely visible gray-on-gray styling.\n\nTarget WCAG-compatible contrast wherever practical.\n\nPrimary text should have strong contrast.\n\nSecondary text should remain comfortably readable.\n\nInteractive controls must be clearly distinguishable from their background.\n\n---\n\n# 4. TEXT COLOR HIERARCHY\n\nCreate a consistent hierarchy.\n\nExample:\n\nHeadline\n\342\206\222 highest emphasis\n\nTitle\n\342\206\222 high emphasis\n\nBody\n\342\206\222 normal emphasis\n\nSecondary information\n\342\206\222 medium emphasis\n\nMetadata\n\342\206\222 lower emphasis\n\nDisabled\n\342\206\222 lowest emphasis\n\nDo not make all text the same brightness.\n\nDo not make secondary text so dark that users cannot read it.\n\n---\n\n# 5. CARDS\n\nReview all cards throughout the app.\n\nIn dark mode:\n\n* Cards must be distinguishable from the page background.\n* Card borders should be subtle.\n* Shadows should not be the primary method of separation.\n* Text must remain readable.\n* Icons must have sufficient contrast.\n* Status indicators must remain visible.\n\nAvoid:\n\nDark background + slightly darker card + dark text.\n\nThat combination makes content unreadable.\n\n---\n\n# 6. BUTTONS\n\nAudit every button.\n\n### Filled buttons\n\nUse a strong primary/container color with correctly contrasting text.\n\n### Outlined buttons\n\nUse visible outlines and readable text.\n\n### Tonal buttons\n\nUse a clearly distinguishable surface.\n\n### Text buttons\n\nEnsure text is not too dark or muted.\n\n### Disabled buttons\n\nMake the disabled state obvious without making the button disappear.\n\nDo not use the same dark surface for both the button and the background.\n\n---\n\n# 7. SWITCHES / TOGGLES\n\nThis is especially important.\n\nAudit every switch.\n\nFor each switch make sure:\n\nON:\n\n* Track is clearly visible.\n* Thumb is clearly visible.\n* Active color is obvious.\n\nOFF:\n\n* Track remains visible.\n* Thumb remains visible.\n* The control does not visually disappear.\n\nDisabled:\n\n* Clearly communicates disabled state.\n\nDo not rely only on color.\n\nThe surrounding label/status should also communicate the state.\n\n---\n\n# 8. FIREWALL STATUS\n\nThe Firewall ON/OFF status is one of the most important elements.\n\nDark mode should make it immediately understandable.\n\nExample:\n\nFirewall ON\n\342\227\217 Active\n\nFirewall OFF\n\342\227\217 Stopped\n\nFirewall Error\n\342\227\217 Needs attention\n\nUse semantic colors carefully:\n\nSuccess\n\342\206\222 active/healthy\n\nError\n\342\206\222 failed/problem\n\nWarning\n\342\206\222 attention required\n\nDo not use overly saturated colors that damage readability.\n\n---\n\n# 9. APP LIST\n\nAudit the installed-app screen carefully.\n\nEach application row should have:\n\n* Clearly visible app name\n* Readable package/details text\n* Visible app icon\n* Clearly visible network status\n* Readable rule summary\n* Proper switch/toggle colors\n* Clear selected/expanded state\n\nExample:\n\nYouTube\nNetwork access\n\nWi-Fi     ON\nMobile    OFF\nBackground OFF\n\nEverything must remain readable in dark mode.\n\n---\n\n# 10. CONNECTION LOG\n\nImprove the dark-mode connection log.\n\nExample:\n\nYouTube\ngooglevideo.com\n443 \342\200\242 TCP\n\nALLOWED\n\nor\n\nBLOCKED\n\nMake sure:\n\n* Destination text is readable.\n* Timestamp is readable.\n* Port/protocol is readable.\n* Allowed/blocked status is highly distinguishable.\n* Long destinations don\'t become visually confusing.\n\nAvoid extremely dark gray metadata.\n\n---\n\n# 11. NAVIGATION\n\nAudit:\n\n* Top app bar\n* Bottom navigation\n* Navigation drawer if present\n* Selected icon\n* Unselected icon\n* Labels\n* Back button\n* Overflow menus\n\nSelected and unselected states must be distinguishable.\n\nUnselected icons should still be clearly visible.\n\nDo not make inactive navigation icons nearly black.\n\n---\n\n# 12. DIALOGS / BOTTOM SHEETS\n\nDark-mode dialogs and bottom sheets must have:\n\n* Appropriate elevated surface\n* Readable title\n* Readable body\n* Clearly visible buttons\n* Correct divider/outline\n* Proper scrim\n* Correct icon colors\n\nAvoid dialogs that become a black rectangle containing dark-gray text.\n\n---\n\n# 13. INPUT FIELDS\n\nAudit every text field.\n\nDark mode should correctly style:\n\n* Background\n* Label\n* Input text\n* Placeholder\n* Cursor\n* Focus indicator\n* Outline\n* Error state\n* Supporting text\n\nFocused fields must be visually obvious.\n\nError text must be readable.\n\nPlaceholder text should be muted but still accessible.\n\n---\n\n# 14. CHIPS / BADGES\n\nAudit all chips and badges.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"Blocked\"\n\"Allowed\"\n\"VPN Active\"\n\"Background\"\n\"Wi-Fi\"\n\"Mobile Data\"\n\nEnsure text and icons have sufficient contrast against their container.\n\n---\n\n# 15. EMPTY STATES\n\nImprove empty screens.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"No blocked apps\"\n\"No recent connections\"\n\"No firewall rules\"\n\nMake sure:\n\n* Illustration/icon is visible\n* Heading is readable\n* Explanation is readable\n* Action button is visible\n\nDo not use low-contrast illustrations.\n\n---\n\n# 16. ERROR / WARNING / SUCCESS STATES\n\nUse semantic colors consistently.\n\nCreate centralized semantic colors such as:\n\nSuccess\nWarning\nError\nInfo\n\nThen use those colors across the application.\n\nDo not choose slightly different green/red/yellow colors on every screen.\n\nThe same state should look the same everywhere.\n\n---\n\n# 17. CHARTS / STATISTICS\n\nAudit charts in dark mode.\n\nMake sure:\n\n* Axes are visible\n* Labels are visible\n* Grid lines are subtle but visible\n* Data lines/bars are distinguishable\n* Legends are readable\n* Tooltips are readable\n\nDo not use chart colors that disappear against the dark surface.\n\n---\n\n# 18. ICONS\n\nAudit every icon.\n\nIcons must use theme-aware colors.\n\nDo not use black icons on dark surfaces.\n\nDo not use white icons everywhere regardless of semantic meaning.\n\nUse:\n\nPrimary icon\nSecondary icon\nDisabled icon\nSuccess icon\nWarning icon\nError icon\n\nconsistently.\n\n---\n\n# 19. DIVIDERS AND BORDERS\n\nDividers must be subtle but visible.\n\nThey should not disappear completely.\n\nUse theme `outlineVariant` or an equivalent semantic color rather than arbitrary gray values.\n\n---\n\n# 20. STATUS BAR / SYSTEM UI\n\nMake dark mode work correctly with Android system UI.\n\nConfigure:\n\n* Status bar\n* Navigation bar\n* System bar icons\n* Edge-to-edge behavior\n\nEnsure system icons remain readable.\n\nDo not use a light status-bar icon configuration on a light surface or the opposite.\n\nRespect Android\'s edge-to-edge requirements.\n\n---\n\n# 21. DYNAMIC COLOR\n\nIf dynamic color is supported, integrate it carefully.\n\nDo not let dynamic colors destroy contrast.\n\nThe NetGuardian semantic colors must remain readable even with different device color palettes.\n\nProvide sensible fallbacks.\n\n---\n\n# 22. THEME ARCHITECTURE\n\nRefactor the theme so that light and dark mode share the same semantic component definitions.\n\nFor example:\n\nNetGuardianTheme\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 LightColorScheme\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 DarkColorScheme\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Typography\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Shapes\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Component styling\n\nComponents should consume semantic theme colors instead of deciding their own colors.\n\n---\n\n# 23. NO HARDCODED DARK-MODE FIXES\n\nSearch the entire project for problematic patterns such as:\n\nColor.Black\nColor.White\nColor.Gray\nColor.DarkGray\n\"#000000\"\n\"#FFFFFF\"\n\nand similar hardcoded colors.\n\nReplace them with theme-aware or semantic colors wherever appropriate.\n\nDo not blindly replace colors that have legitimate branding/illustration purposes.\n\n---\n\n# 24. AUTOMATIC DARK-MODE TESTING\n\nCreate a reusable preview/testing approach so every major component can be evaluated in:\n\nLight mode\nDark mode\n\nTest at minimum:\n\n* Home\n* Apps\n* App details\n* Connections\n* Privacy\n* Settings\n* Onboarding\n* Permission screens\n* Diagnostics\n* Dialogs\n* Bottom sheets\n* Error states\n* Empty states\n\nEnsure text is readable in every state.\n\n---\n\n# 25. ACCESSIBILITY\n\nImprove dark mode accessibility.\n\nSupport:\n\n* Large font sizes\n* Font scaling\n* Content descriptions\n* Touch targets\n* Screen readers\n* Sufficient contrast\n* State communication beyond color alone\n\nDo not communicate \"blocked\", \"allowed\", or \"error\" only through color.\n\nInclude text/icon/state indicators.\n\n---\n\n# 26. VISUAL QUALITY STANDARD\n\nThe final dark mode should feel like a polished premium Android security/privacy application.\n\nVisual direction:\n\n* Clean\n* Modern\n* Professional\n* Calm\n* High readability\n* Strong hierarchy\n* Subtle elevation\n* Consistent spacing\n* Minimal visual noise\n\nAvoid:\n\n* Pure black everywhere\n* Washed-out gray text\n* Random accent colors\n* Excessive glow\n* Excessive borders\n* Low-contrast controls\n* Bright white backgrounds inside dark mode\n* Inconsistent component styling\n\n---\n\n# 27. IMPORTANT\n\nDo NOT redesign the application\'s functionality.\n\nDo NOT remove existing features.\n\nDo NOT significantly alter the light theme.\n\nFocus on:\n\n**Dark-mode correctness + contrast + consistency + accessibility + professional visual quality.**\n\nAfter implementing the new dark theme, perform a full application-wide UI audit and fix every component that still has poor contrast or incorrect colors.\n\nThe final result must have **zero obvious dark-mode readability problems** across the entire application."
