  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Color Schemas**\n\nI\'ve been examining `Theme.kt` and `Color.kt` and have honed in on the key issue. MaterialTheme provides `DarkColorScheme` and `LightColorScheme`, which contain excellent dark colors. I am specifically interested in how this will affect the overall design, now that I have a better understanding of the code.\n\n\n**Addressing Color Discrepancies**\n\nI\'ve discovered a significant inconsistency. While the MaterialTheme offers suitable dark color schemes, UI files directly hardcode color values. Furthermore, I need to define custom semantic color tokens (Success, Warning, Blocked, etc.) that respond to dark/light mode, possibly utilizing `CompositionLocal` or a `NetGuardianColors` extension to adapt colors dynamically. This will provide a single source of truth for all colors and help to make a well-designed dark mode system.\n\n\n**Refining Dark Mode Colors**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific color aspects. I must ensure the theme is used centrally for every screen and component, removing all hardcoded colors like white, black, and gray. I will audit and polish screens, focusing on the dark mode\'s Hero Status Card, Stat Tiles, Activity Preview, and Quick Controls, all while making sure that contrast and accessibility are excellent. I\'m carefully analyzing the contrast of primary and secondary text, badges, borders, switches, and input fields.\n\n\n**Evaluating Dark Mode Readability**\n\nI am now performing a detailed audit of several key screens for dark mode readability. I\'ve pinpointed specific areas in the Apps/Firewall Rules screen (app names, toggle controls), Connections/Live Log screen (log item readability, status indicators), and Privacy/DNS screen (category cards, custom domain entry, DNS selector). I\'m focusing on high contrast active/inactive states and legible text and icons.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Accessibility Enhancements**\n\nI\'m currently addressing specific components for improved dark mode accessibility. This involves ensuring high contrast for icons, labels, and switches within Setting Cards and Toggle Rows, as well as clear instructions and step indicators in the Quick Settings Tile Guide. I\'m focusing on accessible color usage for status indicators in Diagnostics/Permissions and maintaining readability across text fields, dialog buttons, and text within Backup/Restore & Dialogs. Finally, I\'m verifying the Onboarding Wizard\'s visibility. I\'m taking special care to preserve existing light mode aesthetic integrity.\n\n\n**Implementing Custom Colors**\n\nI\'m now integrating custom semantic colors, and I\'ve reviewed the existing `Color.kt` definitions. I am defining new tokens like `onWarning`, `warningContainer`, `onWarningContainer`, `surfaceElevated`, `outlineVariant`, as well as specific colors for badges and cards. I\'m leveraging `LocalExtendedColors` to facilitate access through `MaterialTheme.extendedColors` and am aligning new tokens with existing MaterialTheme properties for seamless dark/light mode switching. I\'m focusing on consistency in this process.\n\n\n**Defining Semantic Colors**\n\nNow, I\'m translating existing color codes into semantic tokens that I can use throughout the application. I\'m focusing on defining colors such as `primaryContainer`, `onPrimaryContainer`, `tertiary` (potentially mapped to a status color), and associated container and on-container colors. I\'m paying careful attention to dark and light mode equivalents. I\'m also ensuring that background and surface colors are properly managed in both modes.\n\n\n**Mapping Color Equivalents**\n\nI\'m now integrating surface and error colors, ensuring consistency across modes. I\'m focusing on defining `surfaceContainer`, `surfaceContainerHigh`, `surfaceContainerHighest` and error-related tokens, including `error`, `onError`, `errorContainer`, and `onErrorContainer`. I am mapping the given hex codes to their corresponding semantic names. I\'m also preparing to incorporate `DarkSafeGreen`, `DarkSuccessContainer`, `DarkOnSuccessContainer`, `LightSafeGreen`, `LightSuccessContainer`, `LightOnSuccessContainer`, `DarkWarningAmber`, `DarkWarningContainer`, `DarkOnWarningContainer`, `LightWarningAmber`, `LightWarningContainer`, and `LightOnWarningContainer` for a cleaner, semantically rich color palette.\n\n\n**Incorporating Blocked Colors**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Blocked color states, creating semantic tokens for dark and light modes. I\'ve defined `DarkBlockedRed`, `DarkBlockedContainer`, `DarkOnBlockedContainer`, and their light mode equivalents. I am adding these to the `NetGuardianSemanticColors` extension in `Theme.kt`, ensuring these blocked state colors are part of a broader, consistent theme. I\'m focusing on ensuring these colors correctly represent blocked states throughout the app.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 93243898
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
