  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Light Mode**\n\nOkay, I\'m currently focused on testing the `HeroStatusCard` in light mode using `NetGuardianTheme`. I\'ve noted that `isSystemInDarkTheme()` defaults to false in tests, which is key. I\'m exploring how this default behavior interacts with my capture approach in `GreetingScreenshotTest.kt`. I am working to ensure the light mode rendering is correct.\n\n\n**Designing Dark Mode Colors**\n\nI\'m now diving into the dark mode color scheme. The goal is a sophisticated, high-contrast, readable design. I am going with a dark cyber/slate palette. I\'ve selected a deep obsidian slate for the background, a refined cool-slate undertone for the surface, and elevated containers with distinct variations. High-contrast sapphire blue for primary is a key element. I am systematically reviewing the color tokens.\n\n\n**Refining Dark Mode Colors**\n\nI\'ve refined the dark mode color tokens further. My aim is to ensure excellent visual contrast and readability. The primary color is `Color(0xFF002F55)`, and the primary container is `Color(0xFF0D3B66)`. High-legibility is key: the `On Primary Container` is `Color(0xFFCEE5FF)`. I\'ve added Secondary, Tertiary, Error and Warning colors. The warning color is `Color(0xFFFBBF24)`.\n\n\n**Expanding Color Palette**\n\nI\'ve been expanding the color palette for both light and dark modes. I\'ve defined specific colors for warning and success states to enhance user feedback. I\'m working to integrate these new tokens into the design, ensuring they\'re readable and aesthetically pleasing across all states. I\'ve paid careful attention to contrast ratios to meet accessibility standards.\n\n\n**Defining Color Tokens**\n\nI am now solidifying the remaining color tokens. I\'ve finished defining the full spectrum for light mode, now I\'m making sure it works. I\'m adding `On Background`, `On Surface` and `On Surface Variant` colors, and reviewing them for contrast. My goal is to create a robust and harmonious visual language.\n\n\n**Mapping Semantic Colors**\n\nI\'m now mapping semantic colors for status badges, pills, buttons, and switches. I\'ve defined a `NetGuardianColors` data class in `Theme.kt`, ensuring a structured approach for light and dark modes. I\'m focused on colors for various states: success, warning, blocked, and allowed, along with general text, surfaces, and switch colors. I am creating visual consistency.\n\n\n**Implementing Color Integration**\n\nI\'m currently working on integrating the new color tokens. I am updating my application\'s semantic color system with `NetGuardianColors`. I\'m using `staticCompositionLocalOf` for easy access. I\'ve noted that several core components across the app were using deprecated and incorrect color tokens in dark mode. My focus is migrating those uses to a consistent system. This will maintain Material 3 standards using `MaterialTheme.colorScheme` as the base.\n\n\n**Applying Color System**\n\nI\'m now applying the color system across components. I\'m updating text styles to use `onSurface`, `onSurfaceVariant`, and `primary`. For cards, I\'m integrating `surface`, `surfaceVariant`, and `outline`. Badges and status indicators are being updated to utilize `netGuardian.*` or `colorScheme.*`. Switches are updated with appropriate colors via `SwitchDefaults`, and text fields using `OutlinedTextFieldDefaults`. FilterChips are updated using the corresponding defaults.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 201166354
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
